The following definition follows the notation of \cite{johnson-madden}; the textile system $\widehat{T}$ is called the dual textile system in \cite[page 15]{nasu}.

\begin{definition} [Inverted textile system]\label{dfn:inverted-textile}
Let $T=(p,q:F \to E)$ be a textile system. Define directed graphs $\widehat{E} = ( E^0 , F^0 , q , p )$  and $\widehat{F} = ( E^1 , F^1 , q , p )$. Then $\hat{p} = ( s_E , s_F)$, $\hat{q} = ( r_E , r_F )$ are graph morphisms from $\widehat{F}$ to $\widehat{E}$ and $\widehat{T}= ( \hat{p} , \hat{q} : \widehat{F} \to \widehat{E}  )$  is a textile system, called the \emph{inverted textile system}. 
\end{definition}

