and $\wt p(\lambda_j^i) = p(\lambda_j)^i$ for all $i, j$.
These formulas agree with the commuting squares of $\Lambda_I$, as asserted by Theorem \ref{lr-insplit-4}.  Indeed, note that 
\[ q^{-1}(\mathcal E^1_v) = \{ \lambda_1\}, \quad q^{-1}( \mathcal E^1_w) = \{ \lambda_2\}, \quad q^{-1}(\mathcal E^2_w) = \{ \lambda_3, \lambda_4\},\]
