In transitional flow stability studies, two main approaches are typically considered: 1) modal analysis \citep{taira2020modal}---has been a leading approach in formulating linear stability theory (LST){,} focusing on eigenvalue analysis of the flow response to initial conditions about its base state and involving infinitesimal disturbances superimposed on a base flow. In LST, stability is defined in the infinite horizon sense, while disregarding short-time perturbation dynamics \citep{schmid2007nonmodal}. 2) Nonmodal analysis---{which} allows focusing on response to external forcing, and {the} study {of} flow behavior in the finite horizon sense, such as the study of transient growth due to the non-normality of the linearized Navier Stokes (LNS) operator \citep{schmid2007nonmodal,karp2017secondary,lozano2021cause}. In particular, in a nonmodal approach {using} input-output formulation, the governing equations are formulated in the form of state equations, allowing the computation of the system's frequency response{,} which contains a great deal of information, and allows a detailed analysis of the response to external forcing. Such tools have been shown to be both computationally efficient and to supply understanding {of} the response of different base flows to actuation. Examples include studies that focused on the externally forced LNS equations to examine dynamic processes, structural features, and energy pathways for a variety of base flows \citep{farrell1993stochastic,bamieh2001energy,jovanovic2004unstable,jovanovic2005componentwise,HC10,mckeon2010critical,mckeon2013experimental,mckeon2017engine,madhusudanan2019coherent,liu2020input,symon2021energy} {and} including our recent studies, where {the} dynamics of large-scale structures in turbulent boundary layer {are} studied by analyzing the flow response to external periodic perturbations \citep{liu2022spatial}, and utilization of input-output approach for modeling different actuation geometry patterns \citep{GG2