\subsection{Choice of norms}

 The selection of the norm plays a critical role in the analysis of input-output behaviors in flow systems. Norms serve as the mathematical infrastructure for quantifying the magnitude of the response to a specific input and provide a basis for defining and computing the amplification between an input and its corresponding output. It is important to note that different choices of norms are associated with the computation of different quantities. For example, the $\mathscr{H}_2$ norm is specifically used to represent perturbation kinetic energy, as referenced in \citep{jovanovic2005componentwise}. In our research, we utilize the $\mathscr{H}_{\infty}$ norm instead of the $\mathscr{H}_2$ norm, \red{which is related to the amplification of one specific mode (which is the most amplified) in the flow, as shown next.} 

 Given the operator \green{$\mathscr{H}(y;k_x,k_z,\omega)$} \blue{after  discretization}, we define the $\mathscr{H}_\infty$ system norm as follows \citep{Zhou1995-dl}:

%\blue{add discussion on norms in introduction so it will be clear why to work with H infinity norm and not H2 norm}.

%%
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:Hinf norm}
\norm{\mathscr{H}}_{\infty}(k_x,k_z) = \sup_{\omega \in \mathbb{R}}{\overline{\sigma}[ \mathscr{H}(y;k_x,k_z,\omega)]},
\end{equation}
%%
and similarly,   \blue{after  discretization of ${\mathscr{H}_\nabla}(k_x,k_z)$ we can obtain}
%%
\begin{equation}
 \label{eq:Hgradinf norm}
\norm{\mathscr{H}_\nabla}_{\infty}(k_x,k_z) = \sup_{\omega \in \mathbb{R}}{\overline{\sigma}[\mathscr{H}_\nabla(y;k_x,k_z,\omega)]}.
\end{equation}
%%
Here, $\overline{\sigma}[ \cdot]$ represents the largest singular value, and $\sup_{\omega \in \mathbb{R}}{}$ represents the supremum operation over all temporal frequencies. %Hence, the $\mathscr{H}_\infty$ norm quantifies the largest singular value of the system, meaning the gain applied to the mode that is most amplified. 
Hence, the $\mathscr{H}_\infty$ norm does not quantify the energy amplification of the whole field, but rather the \ most amplified mode of the system due to harmonic temporal forcing. 
%<****tag222>
%In our results section (Sec.~\ref{sec:Results}), we will demonstrate that the dominant mode of $\norm{\mathscr{H}_\nabla}_{\infty}$ \red{converges at high Reynolds numbers to} the least stable mode (Tolmien-Schlichting wave) of the operator $\mathscr{A}$ \red{(which directly effects the frequency response as shown in Eq. \ref{eq:H_nabla})} that can be identified via classical linear stability analysis. 
%<////tag222>
To account for nonlinear interactions of NSEs, we modify the definition of $\overline{\sigma}[ \cdot]$ as will be shown in the next section.

\subsection{Non-linear frequency response}
 \label{suc:nonlinear}
%<*tag32b>
\teal{In this section, we use structured input-output analysis to model the nonlinear response associated with the full NS system. This modeling of nonlinear interactions will be used in our stability analysis.} 
%</tag32b>
We consider the full NS equations for velocity and pressure perturbations as we seek to incorporate nonlinear behaviors in our analysis. The NSE with no forcing term is presented below:
 %<*tag16b>
\red{
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:NSE}
\begin{matrix}
 \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{u}}{\partial t} = -\boldsymbol{\bar{u}} \cdot \nabla \boldsymbol{u} - \boldsymbol{u} \cdot \nabla \boldsymbol{\bar{u}} - \nabla p + \frac{1}{\Rey} \Delta \boldsymbol{u} - \boldsymbol{u} \cdot \nabla \boldsymbol{u}, \\
\nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{u} = 0.
\end{matrix}
\end{equation}
}
%</tag16b>
Eq. \eqref{eq:NSE} is very similar to Eq. \eqref{eq:LNS}, with the only difference being that the forcing term is swapped by the nonlinear advection term \red{$\boldsymbol{u}\cdot \nabla \boldsymbol{u}$}. Thus, we can model nonlinear interactions as a forcing input to the LNS system. We model the nonlinear advection term in the NSE using the block diagram representation in Fig.~\ref{fig:block_diagram}.
%% 
\begin{figure}
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width = \textwidth]{Unstructured_small_gain_high_res.png}
 \caption{Interconnection loop block diagram for the nonlinear interactions. Adopted from \citet[][Fig. 3]{liu2021}.}
 \label{fig:block_diagram}
\end{figure}
%% 
Here, $\mathbf{u}_{\Xi}$ is the gain applied to the vectorized velocity gradient $\begin{bmatrix} \nabla u & \nabla v & \nabla w \end{bmatrix}^T$ that retains the component-wise structure of the advection term $\boldsymbol{u} \cdot \nabla \boldsymbol{u}$ \citep{liu2021}. %Using Eq. \eqref{eq:H-operator} \blue{(IG: why this equation is used here?)} and \eqref{eq:NSE}, we can find an exact expression for ${\textbf{u}}_\Xi$ that captures the nature of the nonlinear term in the NSE. this expression is
%%
%<*tag24a>
The exact expression for ${\textbf{u}}_\Xi$ that captures the nature of the nonlinear term in the NSE is: 

\red{
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:u_xi}
 {\textbf{u}}_\Xi \equiv \begin{bmatrix}
    -{\textbf{u}}^T & \mathbf{0} & \mathbf{0} \\
    \mathbf{0} & -{\textbf{u}}^T & \mathbf{0} \\
    \mathbf{0} & \mathbf{0} & -{\textbf{u}}^T 
\end{bmatrix}, {\textbf{u}}^T = \begin{bmatrix}
 u(y), v(y), w(y)
 \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation} 
}
\red{Here, $ u(y), v(y), w(y)$ represent} the velocity perturbation field \red{as a function of the wall-normal coordinate $y$}.
%</tag24a>
Thus, \red{${\textbf{u}}_\Xi$} is a matrix representation of the velocity perturbation field that arises when accounting for nonlinear interactions. %\blue{IG: I think the bold text should be placed at the begging of section "2.5. Structures of uncertainty" }
%%
To incorporate the nonlinearity structure, we calculate structured singular values (SSVs) \green{of} the system. SSVs are a generalization of the concept of singular value for the case of a transfer function or a frequency response operator that is feedback-interconnected to an uncertainty matrix, that has a predetermined structure \citep{Zhou1995-dl}. In detail, similarly to the unstructured case, we calculate SSVs for the frequency response operator $\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}$ by solving the following minimization problem \citep{Zhou1995-dl,packard1993}:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Mu}
\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}}[\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}(k_x,k_z,\omega)] = \frac{1}{\text{min}\{ \overline{\sigma} ({\textbf{u}}_\Xi) : {\textbf{u}}_\Xi \in \mathbf{\Delta}, \text{det}[I - \mathscr{H}_{\nabla}(k_x,k_z,\omega) {\textbf{u}}_\Xi] = 0 \}}.
\end{equation}
%%
In eq.~\eqref{eq:Mu}, $\overline{\sigma}(\cdot)$ represents the largest singular value, $\text{det}[\cdot]$ is the determinant of a matrix and $\mathbf{\Delta}$ is a set of matrices, that includes all the matrices that have a certain structure. We quantify amplification with nonlinear interactions that are modeled by an uncertainty matrix ${\textbf{u}}_\Xi \in \mathbf{\Delta}$ using the supremum over all temporal frequencies:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:H_Mu}
\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}}}(k_x,k_z) \equiv \sup_{\omega \in \mathbb{R}}{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}}[ \mathscr{H}_{\nabla}(k_x,k_z,\omega)]},
\end{equation}
%%
this is the "worst case" amplification of perturbation gradients under the uncertainty structure $\mathbf{\Delta}$. This amplification is not an amplification of energy of the whole flow field, but rather the gain applied to the most amplified mode. Thus, we note that $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}}}$ is a generalization of $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\infty}$, for the case of interconnection of \blue{discretized} $\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}$ with an uncertainty matrix ${\textbf{u}}_\Xi \in \mathbf{\Delta}$. Additionally, we use the notation $\norm{\cdot}_{\mu}$ for consistency with previous works \citep{packard1993,liu2021}, despite this operator not being a proper norm {since} it does not satisfy the triangle inequality. 

