%% 
These relations between sets imply that SSV values for a particular set, e.g., \red{$\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta} _ u}$} of the set \red{$\mathbf{\Delta}_u$}, cannot be larger than the SSV values \red{$\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_r}$} obtained from a set of \red{$\mathbf{\Delta}_r$}, if \red{$\mathbf{\Delta}_u$} is its subset. Thus, solving the minimization problem (Eq.~\eqref{eq:Mu}) over these sets of matrices that satisfy the relations in Eq.~\eqref{eq:sets}, yields, 
%% 
%\blue{IG: should the equation below and the corresponding discussion in the last paragraph be updated for the inverse relations? I thought it was done already. Or  maybe should we  keep it that way to be consistent with 2.30?}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:H_mu ineq}
\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{u}}}(k_x,k_z) \leq \norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{r}}}(k_x,k_z) \leq \norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{nr}}}(k_x,k_z) \leq \norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\infty}(k_x,k_z). 
\end{equation}
%%
As we will show next, this hierarchical relationship allows us to explain the difference in the predictions \red{of the most dominant mode} by unstructured and structured input-output methods. Moreover, the norm-like property of the smallest set $\mathbf{\Delta}_{\mathbf{u}}$ is $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{u}}}(k_x,k_z)$, which inverse ($\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{u}}}^{-1} $) would provide the  least tight and most accurate bound to perturbation magnitude to keep the system stable. In particular, the inequality in Eq.~\eqref{eq:small_gain_bound}, can serve as a generalized stability criterion, which is more physically justified to study pathways to transition, in contrast to classical stability theory, in which the mathematical formulation is restricted to infinitesimally small perturbations. However, as mentioned previously, the solution to such a set requires solution of the optimization problem in Eq. \eqref{eq:Mu}, which is elaborate and has not yet been found. The best next approximation of this set would be to use 
$\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{r}}}^{-1}$, which is the most representing bound on velocity perturbation magnitude that we can compute, while $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{nr}}}^{-1}$ and $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\infty}^{-1}$ provide more restricting bounds, respectively. 


\section{Numerical analysis settings}
\label{sec:settings} 

 To compute the operators in Eq.~\eqref{eq:state-space} and the flow field they produce, we apply discretization to the wall-normal direction by using Chebyshev collocation points. We implement this discretization process by using the pseudo-spectral differentiation matrices from \citet{weideman2000matlab} for differentiation in the wall-normal direction. Boundary conditions were implemented based on \citet{trefethen2000spectral}. {In particular, we employ a quasi-parallel assumption and use the change of variables described in \citet{schmid2002stability} to transform the bounded domain $\begin{bmatrix} -1, & 1 \end{bmatrix}$ to the semi-infinite domain $\begin{bmatrix} 0, & \infty \end{bmatrix}$ of the flat plate boundary layer (for details see Appendix A.4 in \citet{schmid2002stability}). We set the wall-normal domain range to $[0, L_y]=\begin{bmatrix} 0, & 15 \end{bmatrix}$ as this height is well within the free stream and thus captures the entire base flow profile variation. 
 All of the calculations are performed in $\text{MATLAB}^\text{®}$ R2023b. We denote by $N_y$ the number of Chebyshev collocation points chosen for the discretization process. For the wave-number domain, we have used a $50\times90$ grid of logarithmically spaced values with the following boundaries: $(k_{x_{min}}=10^{-4},k_{x_{max}}=3.02)$ and $(k_{z_{min}}=10^{-2},k_{z_{max}}=15.84)$. This is the same grid used in \citep{jovanovic2005componentwise}. A Chebyshev collocation method with $N_y=\red{61}$ points was used for discretization along the wall-normal direction in all computations and for all base flows. This spacing was determined to be adequate for obtaining reliable results, as doubling the number of source points did not affect the trends and values of the curves, nor did it cause any noticeable changes in the \green{contour plots presented in \S\ref{sec:Results}}. 

In terms of temporal frequencies, an adaptive approach was applied using MATLAB, utilizing the highly computationally efficient \textit{Sigma} and \textit{mussv} functions. The results obtained from this adaptive method were compared to those obtained using a constant grid of frequency values. It was demonstrated that the results from the constant grid approach were converging to those achieved by adaptively selecting temporal frequencies.

The values of the $\mathscr{H}_{\infty}$ norm were calculated by using the MATLAB function \textit{hinfnorm}, which is part of the Robust Control Toolbox \citep{balas2007robust}. Additionally, We calculate \blue{the estimated value of $\norm{ \mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu}$} for the case of ${\textbf{u}}_\Xi \in \mathbf{\Delta}_{nr}$ using the function \textit{mussv} in the Robust Control Toolbox \citep{balas2007robust} and we use the numerical algorithm of Generalized power iteration for finding lower bounds on the SSV \citep{mushtaq2023} for the case of ${\textbf{u}}_\Xi \in \mathbf{\Delta}_{r}$.

