%In this study, we have utilized tools from robust control theory: first, to derive a novel stability criterion for shear flows \red{depending on perturbation magnitude}; second, to characterize the \red{most dominant} flow structures in transitional boundary layers---Couette flow, \red{plane Poiseuille}, and Blasius flow---that arise for a wide range of Reynolds numbers. 


In classical linear stability theory, the critical Reynolds number is determined for an infinitesimally small disturbance, which triggers the flow to become unstable. 
However, it is well known from experiments that the flow can become unstable at higher \citep{pfenninger1961transition,nishioka1975experimental,avila2023transition} or lower Reynolds numbers \citep{nishioka1985some,asai1995boundary,burin2012subcritical,sano2016universal,klotz2017couette,Huang_Gao_Gao_Xi_2024} than the critical Reynolds number computed via classical linear stability theory for infinitesimally small disturbances. It illustrates that disturbance magnitude has an impact on the stability of flows, and thus, the stability criterion should account for finite disturbance magnitude. 
%%
Herein, we propose a novel stability criterion based on the \emph{small gain theorem} coupled with unstructured and structured input-output analyses. The criterion provides an upper bound---that is determined by computing the inverse of the norm-like quantities via unstructured and structured input-output analyses---for velocity perturbations that ensure stability. % in the \red{infinite horizon} sense. 
%<*tag21c>%IG
\red{Our stability criterion does not guarantee a transition, but merely provides an estimate of a threshold for disturbance magnitude that will trigger flow instability. This threshold can be reached via different transition routes, such as through the existence of a finite-size perturbation in the flow, triggering transition via a bypass transition scenario, or via a transient growth scenario where a set of infinitesimal perturbations leads to perturbation amplitude amplification that can surpass the estimated threshold by our approach.  We demonstrate in our study that the provided threshold using the structured case is physically more justified and agrees better with both experimental and DNS observations.
 }
%</tag21c>%IG
%<*tag14cc>
In particular, we employ these input-output approaches to compute the following 
 quantities:  $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\infty}^{-1}$, \red{which does not take into account the structure of the nonlinear term in the Navier-Stokes equations;} whereas the quantity  $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{u}}}^{-1}$ that used for \red{reproducing this structure of the nonlinear advection term}, is estimated via  $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{nr}}}^{-1}$ and  $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{r}}}$. Here,  $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{nr}}}^{-1}$ is obtained via structured input-output with non-repeating uncertainty \red{\citep{liu2021,shuai2023structured}}---in which a block diagonal matrix with three different blocks is feedback-interconnected to the frequency response operator associated with the LNS operator. We also obtained  $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{r}}}^{-1}$ via structured input-output with repeating uncertainty \red{\citep{mushtaq2023,mushtaq2024structured}}---in which a block diagonal matrix with three repeating blocks is feedback-interconnected to the frequency response operator associated with the LNS operator. 
%</tag14cc>
We show that \blue{the imposed threshold that determined from these inverse of }norm-like quantities obtained by these three methods comply with a hierarchical relationship that we obtained in Eq.~\eqref{eq:H_mu ineq}, \blue{in which the unstructured case is the most conservative, imposing the lowest bound on disturbance magnitude,} which holds for all Reynolds numbers. 
%<*tag133>
Based on our hierarchical relation and the results, we also show that structured input-output with repeating uncertainty analysis, provides the \blue{closest bound}  to \red{$\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{u}}}^{-1}$, which incorporates the accurate structure of the nonlinear term in the NSE} (\red{as} defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:small_gain_bound}) on disturbances to sustain stability. 
%</tag133>

\textit{Our framework addresses the discrepancy between the computed critical Reynolds via modal and non-modal approaches in previous studies and the documented Reynolds numbers 
at which transition is observed in experiments.} Stability analysis with our approach is in agreement with previous experimental work, which found that transition can occur at lower Reynolds numbers than the critical Reynolds number predicted by LST, and due to finite amplitude exceeding a certain bound, which{, with our approach,} we can quantify at any Reynolds number. In particular, we address the nonexistence of a critical Reynolds number predicted by LST for plane Couette flow. We use our novel stability criterion to show that Couette flow is indeed unstable in the infinite horizon sense and can transition to turbulence at a wide range of Reynolds numbers, as supported by experimental results \citep{nishioka1985some,klotz2017couette,burin2012subcritical}. 
%<*tag31b>
\teal{For all the  Reynolds numbers considered in this work, our analysis shows that there is a finite perturbation amplitude that would trigger the instability of Couette flow. 
%This leads to the conclusion of an inherently nonlinear instability mechanism, which requires perturbations of finite magnitude. 
This conclusion is supported by DNS works which use nonlinear optimization to obtain the optimal perturbation in Couette flow \citep{reddy1998stability,duguet2010towards,duguet2013minimal}. }
%This is in contrast to both plane Poiseuille and Blasius flows, where instability can be driven by a linear mechanism and in the presence of infinitesimally small perturbations.}
%</tag31b>



%%
%We use this criterion to analyze the instability of Couette, plane Poiseuille, and Blasius flows for a wide range of Reynolds numbers. 


%<*tag222>
We show that \red{when only infinitesimally small perturbations are present in the flow and transient growth is not triggered}, our analysis converges to LST results, i.e., predicting TS mode triggering instability at the critical Reynolds number that LST predicts. It is not a coincidence, but it will not always be true.  LST is based on eigenvalue analysis, which identifies the TS mode as the least stable eigenvalue of the system, and the critical Reynolds number is the Reynolds number at which the \red{growth rate of the} TS mode \red{becomes positive}. 
%% 
\red{In our analysis, the dominant mode for a given Reynolds number is the mode that sets the lowest threshold on velocity perturbation magnitude to trigger instability. This mode is not necessarily identical to the least stable mode predicted by LST, defined by the largest growth rate. At Reynolds numbers below the critical value, our analysis shows that the TS mode is not the dominant mode, as the threshold is initially set by SPS (in unstructured case) or SPS and later DLR mode in structured cases. However, being the least stable,  the TS mode experiences explosive growth, which in turn, makes  it the dominant mode that sets the threshold for disturbance magnitude at the proximity of the critical Reynolds number for both plane Poiseuille and Blasius flows according to structured and unstructured cases.}
%</tag222>

\red{For  the unstructured case, using $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\infty}^{-1}$ we reproduce the stability diagrams that converge to LST, where inside the neutral curve the flow is unstable for any infinitesimal disturbance.} By \red{imposing a structure for the nonlinear advection term} via a structured input-output approach, using the norm-like properties$\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{r}}}$ and $\norm{\mathscr{H}_{\nabla}}_{\mu_{\mathbf{\Delta}_{nr}}}$, our analysis provides an upper bound of a finite value for the disturbance that varies with the Reynolds number, under which the flow remains stable. \teal{This finite bound corresponds to a stable region inside the neutral stability curve predicted by LST, given sufficiently small perturbations for both plane Poiseuille and Blasius flows. The obtained threshold  is in agreement with the disturbance level observations in the experimental study  \cite{nishioka1975experimental}  for which the flows remained laminar at post-critical Reynolds numbers in plane channel flow.} \blue{However, this result should be treated with caution.  small gain theorem guarantees stability only for cases where the nominal system (i.e., the linear interactions represented by $\mathscr{H}_\nabla$), is stable. LST predicts that the linear system is unstable inside the neutral loop, due to an exponentially growing mode. Thus, our analysis holds only in the case that the system inside the neutral curve region is initially stable, which may be due to non-linear diffusion mechanisms that, to some extent, should be represented in the feedback interconnected system in a structured input-output formulation. Furthermore, in spatially evolving, i.e., non-parallel, base flows such as the Blasius boundary layer, the Reynolds number increases  as the disturbance propagates downstream. This might cause a disturbance of any size to cross the unstable region where an infinitesimal threshold around the perimeter of the neutral curve will trigger instability of the flow. %Therefore, it is unlikely to observe a transition at the postcritical Reynolds number for Blaisus flow.
}


We also use these three input-output approaches to   elucidate \teal{the interplay between the dominant modes that set the threshold in our stability criterion on disturbance magnitude}. We conducted our analysis on three canonical flows---Couette, \red{plane Poiseuille}, and Blasius for a wide range of Reynolds numbers, which in turn allowed us to obtain new insights on the interplay between flow structures that govern the transition physics of wall-bounded shear flows, which were elusive in past studies. \blue{We demonstrate that there is an interplay between flow structures such as streaks, oblique modes, and TS wave that govern the flow physics for different Reynolds numbers, where either oblique \citep{liu2021,shuai2023structured} and streak modes \citep{reddy1993energy,schmid1994optimal,schmid2000linear,bamieh2001energy,jovanovic2004modeling,jovanovic2004unstable,jovanovic2005componentwise} govern the flow at subcritical Reynolds number, setting a finite size threshold on disturbance magnitude,  whereas upon the critical value the TS mode dominates and sets the threshold such that any infinitesimal disturbance would trigger transition at the critical Reynolds value predicted by LST. This is due to the fact, as explained before,  that at the proximity of the critical Reynolds number, LST shows that the TS mode becomes unstable close to the critical Reynolds number based on eigen mode analysis, it grows rapidly and overpowers other modes that in turn sets the threshold on disturbance magnitude based on our stability approach. 
%Lastly, we observe a different physical mechanism that may trigger instability in Couette flow compared to \red{plane Poiseuille} and Blasius flow. While both \red{plane Poiseuille} and Blasius flow display a dominant TS wave structure at high Reynolds numbers, which triggers instability, in Couette flow, a monotonic increase of all modes is observed.
}



