Wavelet transforms are a powerful tool for analyzing signals with non-stationary or time-varying frequency content, as they simultaneously localize frequency components in both time and frequency domains. This  makes them  particularly well-suitable for wastewater surveillance time series, which often exhibit a mixture of long-term epidemiological trends and short-term fluctuations arising from sampling variability, laboratory processing, and environmental noise. 

In this study, we employed discrete wavelet analysis to systematically compare signal characteristics between different wastewater sample types. This approach enabled us to separate long-term trends from short-term variability and to investigate sample-type-specific patterns in SARS-CoV-2 wastewater signals.

In the following sections, we briefly present the theoretical foundation of wavelet transforms and outline our implementation of discrete wavelet analysis for comparing signal structures across sample types (See Mallat, 1999, for a full theoretical overview).


\subsection{Wavelet transform}
A wavelet transform (WT) is the decomposition of a signal into a set of basis functions consisting of contractions, expansions, and translations of a mother function $\psi(t)$, called the wavelet.
The continuous wavelet transform (CWT) of a signal $x(t)$ is defined as:

$$W_x (s,\tau)= \frac{1}{\sqrt s}\int_{-\infty}^\infty x(t)\psi^*_{s,\tau}(t)dt,$$

where $$\psi_{s,\tau}(t)=\frac{1}{\sqrt s} \psi(\frac{t-\tau}{s})$$

is a scaled and translated version of  $\psi(t)$, and  $\psi^*$ denotes its complex conjugate. The scale parameter $s$ controls frequency resolution, while the translation parameter $\tau$ controls temporal localization.

%Although CWT provides a detailed, redundant analysis over continuous scales and shifts, this redundancy can be computationally intensive and less efficient for tasks such as compression or feature extraction.  In contrast, the Discrete Wavelet Transform (DWT) offers a compact, non-redundant representation.  The discrete wavelet transform (DWT) addresses this by using dyadic sampling (i.e., $s = 2^j$, $\tau = k \cdot 2^j$), yielding an orthogonal, non-redundant representation that is computationally efficient and well-suited for denoising and preserving signal structure.

Although the CWT offers a fine-grained and redundant representation, it is computationally intensive. The discrete wavelet transform (DWT) addresses this by restricting the scale and translation parameters to dyadic values ($s=2^j$, $\tau=k\cdot 2^j$), yielding an orthogonal, non-redundant representation that is computationally efficient and particularly effective for denoising and structure preservation.


\subsection{Discrete Wavelet Reconstruction}
The DWT represents a discrete time series  $x[n]$ as a set of wavelet coefficients sampled from the CWT. These coefficients form an orthogonal or biorthogonal basis, typically defined on a dyadic time-frequency grid. 

For orthogonal wavelets,  the basis functions take the form:

$$\psi_{j,k}(t)= 2^{j/2} \psi(2^jt-k),$$

where $j$ controls the scale (frequency) and $k$ determines the position (time). This formulation enables multiresolution analysis, in which each level $j$ captures the signal’s structure in a specific frequency band, while translations $k$ provide temporal localization. The dyadic structure allows for efficient, hierarchical signal decomposition using filter banks.

In practice, the DWT is implemented by recursively applying a pair of finite impulse response  filters. The low-pass filter $g[n]$ captures the coarse-scale or approximation features of the signal, while the high-pass filter $h[n]$ extracts fine-scale or detail features. At each decomposition level $l$, the signal is passed through these filters, followed by downsampling by a factor of 2. This yields two sets of coefficients:
$$cA_l[n] = \sum_k cA_{l-1}[k] \cdot g[2n - k], \; cD_l[n] = \sum_k cA_{l-1}[k] \cdot h[2n - k]$$
with $cA_0=x[n]$, the original signal.  

The approximation coefficients $cA_l[n]$ reflect the smoothed, long-term components of the signal, while the detail coefficients $cD_l[n]$ capture high-frequency, transient variations at level $l$.



