\begin{abstract}
Inspired by the growth dynamics of the protist \textit{Physarum polycephalum}, we use a formalism that describes adaptive incompressible Hagen-Poiseuille flows on channel networks to identify graphs connecting different nodes within Euclidean space. These graphs are either suboptimal or optimal in terms of their length. Occasionally, we obtain graph tree configurations that are topologically equivalent to Steiner trees. This approach is employed to design communication systems, such as railway networks, motorways, or fibre webs. As a proof of concept, we explicitly apply this framework to the Portuguese railway network.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Several classes of equations utilising Hagen-Poiseuille (H-P) flows have been developed to describe the growth of the protist \textit{Physarum polycephalum}, commonly known as slime mould. Despite lacking a neural system or centralised control, this organism exhibits advanced behaviour, such as selecting the shortest path between food sources in mazes \cite{maze}. With multiple food sources, \textit{Physarum} can construct networks with comparable efficiency, fault tolerance, and cost to artificial networks \cite{tero3, tero_tokyo}. Various biological experiments indicate that the network tree structures of slime mould often resemble Steiner trees. Algorithms have been created using adaptive H-P type flows to interpret both \textit{Physarum} and Steiner tree formation \cite{tero_steiner, sun_physarum_steiner, rodrigo_tese, hsu_steiner_physarum}. The Steiner tree represents a network optimisation problem in graphs and has significant applications in physics, biology, and engineering \cite{sun_physarum_drugs, travelling_salesman}. 

The survival of multicellular and multinucleated organisms relies on their network of veins. A vein network must efficiently transport and distribute resources and nutrients throughout the body for optimal survival, providing short pathways between important locations. To ensure transport efficiency, a biological network should be as short as possible to minimise the costs of building the network and energy dissipation. Furthermore, biological networks need to be robust and redundant to prevent failure if a channel is damaged. In the case of \textit{Physarum}, its vein network adaptability is vital for foraging and survival.

Previously developed models, based on the phenomenology of adaptive H-P flows to study the formation and optimisation of network paths of \textit{Physarum}, relied on deterministic formalism to solve systems of ordinary differential equations on graphs \cite{transport1, rodrigo_artigo}. These graphs cover bounded regions of Euclidean space. The vertices of the graph can represent branching points of veins or edges, as well as sources or sinks of an incompressible fluid. Given the initial inflows and outflows at the sources and sinks, along with the total fluid volume in the network, and assuming that the radius of the network veins is elastic and can adapt as a function of the flux, the flow on the graph reaches a steady state where some veins transport fluid while others do not. By maintaining constant inflows and outflows over time, a steady state is achieved with a fixed subgraph or tree geometry. The steady states are not unique and depend on the initial conditions of the adapted conductivities. A particularity of this approach is that if one of the connections in the steady-state subgraph is inactivated, another graph link is formed, illustrating the adaptability and resilience of this adaptation process \cite{Val2}. This geometric construction mimics several natural systems sensitive to the spatial distribution of sources, sinks, and local flows.


In this paper, we analyse extremal length graphs connecting $n$ points in Euclidean space using the properties of adaptive incompressible H-P flows that preserve fluid volume in the network \cite{rodrigo_tese,rodrigo_artigo, Val, Val2}. To simulate communication systems and achieve a level of variability that may describe the resilient properties of natural flows, we have introduced several stochastic actualisation processes over time, leading to suboptimal or optimal solutions for graph networks. In all the cases tested, we have consistently obtained steady solutions that are topologically equivalent to Steiner tree solutions, which can then be further optimised relative to the graph length. We have derived suboptimal graph solutions for an ideal Portuguese railway network connecting all the main cities and land and sea borders.


\section{Finding paths of extremal lengths}

Consider a point lattice within a bounded region of Euclidean space of dimension $\mathcal{N}$. The point lattice can be represented by an undirected graph $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V},E)$, where $\mathcal{V}$ is the set of vertices and $E$ is the set of edges. Given a bounded domain $\mathcal{D}$ of Euclidean space, we can construct the graph  $\mathcal{G}$ through a Delaunay triangulation of a set of points with coordinates $(x_i^1,...,x_i^\mathcal{N})$, with $i = 1, ..., N$. Suppose $N$ is sufficiently large and well distributed within $\mathcal{D}$. In that case, we can make the area of triangles sufficiently small and approximate any Euclidean path by a collection of straight edges $(i,j)\in E$ connecting nodes $i$ and $j$. The graph $\mathcal{G}$ is connected, having a single component. The formalism established here is very general, though we assume that $\mathcal{N}=2$. 

From the graph $\mathcal{G}$, we choose $n<<N$ points.  The set of $n$ points may represent the sources and sinks of a fluid injected into the network. Thus, the graph illustrates a network of channels through which an incompressible fluid circulates. Fluid is injected from the sources and exits through the sinks. The inflow and outflow are balanced, resulting in zero net flow.
Our primary objective is to investigate these types of flows, further assuming that the edges of $\mathcal{G}$ can adjust such that they are capable of either conducting or not conducting the fluid.

The question is, under what conditions does this flow adapt to an optimised network comprising a subset of conducting edges that connect the $n$ points of the graph $\mathcal{G}$? 

For example, consider the case where $n=3$ in a bounded region of the Euclidean plane. The three points are arranged in a triangular configuration. Two possible graphs connect the three points, as illustrated in figure \ref{fig:triangle_theo}. The coordinates of the three points are
\begin{equation}
p_1 = (0,0),\ \ p_2 = (1,0),\ \  p_3 = \left(\frac{1}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right). 
\label{points}
\end{equation}
A tree or closed graph can connect these three points, as depicted in figure \ref{fig:triangle_theo}.

By definition, the Steiner tree of a set of points on the Euclidean plane is a tree of minimal length that connects all the points. The graph in figure~\ref{fig:triangle_theo}a) has length $L_a = 3$, and in b), $L_b = 3\sqrt{7}/4\approx1.98$. The graph in figure \ref{fig:triangle_theo}b) represents the tree of minimal lengths connecting the three points --- the Steiner tree connecting these points. In the Steiner tree, there exists a fourth point with coordinates $p_S = \left(\frac{1}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}\right)$. The challenge in constructing Steiner trees is that, in some instances, there are additional points --- Steiner points --- that, along with the initial points, span the tree of minimal length.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
 \includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{fig1}
\caption{Two possible graphs connecting the three points \eqref{points}. a) A graph connecting the three points in the plane. b) The Steiner tree of minimal length connecting the three points in the plane. In this case, the tree has an additional point --- the Steiner point $p_S$.}
\label{fig:triangle_theo}
\end{figure}


There are two issues in constructing a Steiner tree. The first is determining the number of Steiner points that organise the Steiner tree. The second is identifying which edges connect the initial data points to the Steiner points. If we know these for a configuration of $n$ points, the coordinates of the Steiner points can be easily obtained by solving linear systems of equations involving the unknown coordinates of the Steiner points \cite{Val}.

The tree connecting the three points can also represent many physical, social, or biological systems. For example, it can act as a model of a road network linking three cities. Assume each road is used by travellers moving from city $i$ to city $j$, and the number of travellers going from $i$ to $j$, where $i\neq j$, is roughly the same for every $(i,j)$ pair. For the road network shown in the graph of figure 
\ref{fig:triangle_theo}b), the average distance between two cities is $L_b = 1.98$, whereas for the network in figure \ref{fig:triangle_theo}a), the average distance is $L_a = 1$. From a communication perspective, the configuration in figure \ref{fig:triangle_theo}a) is more beneficial for users due to its lower transportation costs. However, its construction cost is higher. In section~\ref{sec4}, we define the optimisation criteria for analysing communication systems.



\section{Adaptive incompressible Hagen-Poiseuille flows on graphs} \label{sec:adaptive-hp}

Specific systems, such as blood vessels or \textit{Physarum}, can be modelled by a class of equations that describe the steady flow of viscous incompressible fluids in networks made up of straight channels with multiple sources and sinks, and adaptive conductivities \cite{rubinow_book,  transport1, Val2}. The flow is similar to Hagen-Poiseuille flow, and the fluid volume within the network stays constant \cite{rodrigo_artigo}.

A network of veins is represented by an undirected graph subset of $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V},E)$. 
The network has $K$ sources and $R$ sinks located at fixed nodes, and $n=K+R$. The fluid flux from outside the network to the inside (and vice versa) at node $j$ is denoted by $ S_j$. If the network has a source at node $j$, then $S_j > 0$; if it has a sink at node $j$, $S_j < 0$; otherwise, $S_j = 0$. Given that the fluid is incompressible,
$$
    \sum_{j = 1}^n S_j = \sum_{j:\text{sources}}S_j + \sum_{j:\text{sinks}}S_j = 0,
$$
and the volume of fluid in the system remains constant.

The steady state of the flow within the network is determined by Kirchhoff's law, which states that flux is conserved at each node $j$,
\begin{equation} \label{eq:kirchhoff}
    \sum_{j:(i,j)\in E} Q_{ij} =\sum_{j:(i,j)\in E} D_{ij}\frac{(p_i-p_j)}{L_{ij}} =S_i \quad , i = 1, ..., N,
\end{equation}
where $p_i$ is the pressure at node $i$, $L_{ij}$ is the length of the graph edge connecting nodes $i$ and $j$, and $D_{ij}$ is the conductivity of the edge $(i,j)$.

The edges represent elastic cylindrical channels that can expand or contract transversely to the flow. The edge connecting node $i$ to node $j$ has a fixed length $L_{ij}$ and a variable radius $r_{ij}$; the edge $(i,j)$ has (variable) conductivity $D_{ij}$ given by $D_{ij} = \pi r_{ij}^4/8\eta$, with $\eta$ the dynamic viscosity of the fluid. The volume of the channel connecting node $i$ to node $j$ is given by $V_{ij} = \pi r_{ij}^2 L_{ij} = \sqrt{8\pi\eta}L_{ij}\sqrt{D_{ij}}$, \cite{Lan}. As the fluid is incompressible, the volume of fluid in the network remains constant and is given by 
$$
V = \sum_{(i,j)\in E} V_{ij} = \beta \sum_{(i,j)\in E} L_{ij}\sqrt{D_{ij}},
$$
where $\beta = \sqrt{8\pi\eta}$.

The formalism developed in 
\cite{rodrigo_artigo} states that the conductivity of the network's channels follows the adaptation law
\begin{equation} \label{eq:dij_adaptation}
    \frac{d}{dt}\sqrt{D_{ij}} = \alpha \frac{g(Q_{ij})}{\sum_{(k,m)\in E} L_{km}\, g(Q_{km})} - \sqrt{D_{ij}} \quad,\quad (i,j)\in E,
\end{equation}
where $g(\cdot)$ is an arbitrary function describing the elasticity of the channels, $\alpha = V/\beta$, and $t$ is a dimensionless time. 
The function $g(\cdot)$, is obtained by  minimising the dissipated energy per unit time of the H-P flow, $\mathcal{P} = \sum_{(i,j)\in E}Q_{ij}^2 L_{ij} / D_{ij}$, relative to $\sqrt{D_{ij}}$, resulting in
\begin{equation} \label{eq:g_minimum}
    g(Q_{ij}) = Q_{ij}^{2/3}.
\end{equation}


To derive the steady-state solution of the adaptive incompressible H-P flow on a graph, we choose a set of $n$ sources and sinks, and we initialise all the edges of the graph $\mathcal{G}$ with conductivities $D_{ij}(t=0)>0$. These conductivities remain positive for all the edges of $\mathcal{G}$.
To describe the temporal evolution of the radii of the channels/edges of $\mathcal{G}$,  we first solve the linear equations outlined in equation \eqref{eq:kirchhoff} relative to the pressures. The fluxes $Q_{ij}$ are then determined by the same equations \eqref{eq:kirchhoff}. The channel conductivities are adapted in accordance with equations \eqref{eq:dij_adaptation}-\eqref{eq:g_minimum}. These equations \label{eq:dij_adaptation} are integrated using the Euler method with an integration time step of $\Delta t$. These steps are repeated over time, with $\Delta t$ time intervals.

Suppose the sources and sinks remain constant over time. In that case, the adaptation process converges to a steady state of the channel conductivities, and a subgraph of $\mathcal{G}$ is formed with conductivities exceeding a certain threshold. This steady state depends on the initial conductivities, and the subgraph may be disconnected, \cite{rodrigo_tese}.

To simulate communication systems where the roles of sources or sinks change over time, the conductivity values $D_{ij}$ do not settle at a stable value and vary with each iteration. However, the shape of the steady-state graph maintains a fixed geometry. 

To simulate all possible itineraries between the nodes of a network and determine a steady-state path of the embedded subgraph in the initial graph $\mathcal{G}$, we utilise four different stochastic algorithms to update the conductivities of the channels and to alter the roles of the source/sink characteristics of the nodes. We consider the following updating algorithms:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \texttt{Random pair}: At each iteration, a source (with $S_i = I_0$) and a sink (with $S_j = -I_0$) are selected randomly from among the $n$ sites.
    \item \texttt{Random half}: At each iteration, $n/2$ sources (each with $S_i = I_0/(\frac{n}{2})$) and $n/2$ sinks (each with $S_j = -I_0/(\frac{n}{2})$) are randomly selected from the $n$ sites; if $n$ is an odd number, one site is considered inactive ($S_j=0$).
    \item \texttt{Random random}: At each iteration, $1 \leq n_{so} \leq n - 1$ sources are randomly selected from the $n$ sites (each with $S_i = I_0/n_{so}$), and the remaining $n-n_{so}$ sites act as sinks (each with $S_j = -I_0/(n-n_{so})$).
    \item \texttt{Random source}: At each iteration, a source (with $S_i = I_0$) is randomly selected from the $n$ sites, while the remaining $n - 1$ sites serve as sinks (each with $S_j = - I_0 / (n-1)$).
\end{enumerate}


Different initial conductivity values lead to distinct steady-state graph or tree shapes. A stochastic search will generate various trees with different shapes and lengths. All simulations were conducted $N_{\text{runs}}$ times, each with unique random initial conditions for every configuration with $n$ sites.

The adaptive H-P flow equations require at least one source node and one sink node for a tree to form. In this paper, for a configuration with $n$ sites, each site is equivalent to every other site, making each site equally likely to be a source or a sink. Different stochastic algorithms may be employed to determine which nodes function as sources and which nodes act as sinks at each iteration step.


\section{Optimisation criteria}\label{sec4}

Two metrics will characterise each steady-state subgraph: its length $L$, and the average length $\nu$ between all pairs of sites, $(i,j)$.

The length $L$ of a graph is defined by  
\begin{equation} \label{eq:L}
    L = \sum_{(i,j)\in E'} L_{ij},
\end{equation}
where $E'$ is the set of effectively conducting edges; that is, edges with conductivity values above a certain threshold, $E' = \{(i,j)\in E: D_{ij} > D_{\text{thresh}}\}$, with $D_{\text{thresh}} = 10^{-4}$. 

The average length $\nu$ between pairs of sites  is given by 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:nu}
    \nu = \frac{1}{\left(^n_2\right)} \sum^{\left(^n_2\right)}_{(i,j)\in E'} L_{s_i s_j} = \frac{2}{n(n-1)}\sum^{\left(^n_2\right)}_{(i,j)\in E'} L_{s_i s_j},
\end{equation}
where $L_{s_i s_j}$ is the shortest path length between the two sites $i$ and $j$, with $D_{ij} > D_{\text{thresh}}$.  


From the point of view of the user, an optimal subgraph that connects all the sources and sinks should exhibit a low $\nu$ value, while a subgraph with a low $L$ has lower construction and maintenance costs.

Our aim is to create algorithms that help with decisions about the design of communication systems such as roads, railways, or fibre networks.

\section{Results}

To examine the algorithms and parameters described, the simplest case considered is a network represented by a graph embedded in two-dimensional Euclidean space, resulting from a Delaunay triangulation. The nodes form a square point lattice, with positions randomly perturbed by Gaussian noise with a standard deviation of $\sigma = 0.5$. The square point lattice has a side length of $1$ and contains $N = N'\times N'$ nodes. Among these nodes, we select $n$ nodes to serve as sources and sinks.

The parameters for the adaptive incompressible H-P flow are: $\beta = \alpha = 1$, $I_0 = 1$, $V = 100$, $N' = 25$, $\Delta t = 0.1$, $N_{\text{runs}} = 300$.

The cases of $n = \{3, 4, 5\}$ will be examined, with the sites arranged in a regular polygon configuration. A real transport network, the Portuguese railway system, will also be analysed.

