\subsection{Triangular configuration}

For $n = 3$, we chose sites positioned in an equilateral triangle with sides measuring $\ell=0.8$. The triangle's perimeter is $P_\triangle = 2.4$, and the length of the minimal Steiner tree is $L_{\text{Stei}_\triangle} = 1.386$.

The steady-state results for the \texttt{Random pair} and \texttt{Random random} algorithms are shown in figure~\ref{fig:tri_random}. The optimised configuration for a communication system is the  \texttt{Random pair} solution. The minimum Steiner tree configuration was achieved using the \texttt{Random random} algorithm. 



\subsection{Square configuration}

For $n = 4$, we selected sites arranged in a square with sides of length $\ell=0.7$. The perimeter of the square is $P_{\square} = 2.8$, and the minimum Steiner tree length is $L_{\text{Stei}_\square} = 1.912$.

The results for the  \texttt{Random pair} and \texttt{Random half} algorithms are shown in figure \ref{fig:square_random}. The displayed results represent the steady states obtained with the smallest values of $L$ and $\nu$ values for each algorithm, along with the graph of $\nu$ as a function of $L$ for all $N_{\text{runs}} = 300$ runs conducted for each algorithm.

The \texttt{Random pair} algorithm did not provide a solution topologically equivalent to a Steiner tree. The \texttt{Random half} algorithm reveals two suboptimal Steiner-like solutions. Nevertheless, the geometry with lower communication costs from the user's perspective is the square.


