\subsection{Pentagonal configuration}

For $n = 5$, we selected sites arranged in a pentagon with sides of length $\ell=0.55$. The perimeter of the pentagon is $P_{\pentago} = 2.75$, and the minimum Steiner tree length is $L_{\text{Stei}_{\pentago}} = 2.14$.

The results for the \texttt{Random source} algorithm are shown in figure \ref{fig:penta_random source}. These results represent the steady states achieved with the smallest values of $L$ and $\nu$ values and the graph of $\nu$ as a function of $L$ for all $N_{\text{runs}} = 300$ runs.

The tree with the smallest $\nu$ for these three algorithms has a length equal to the perimeter of the square. The \texttt{Random source} algorithm also generated a Steiner tree-like solution.




