\subsection{Portugal communication systems}

The algorithms described will now be used to approximate the mainland Portuguese railway transport network. The mesh resembles mainland Portugal and comprises 1005 nodes and 2817 edges, resulting from a Delaunay triangulation \cite{rodrigo_tese}. We chose 25 sites of interest: the 18 Portuguese district capitals and seven other major cities. The length of each edge $L_{ij}$ was the geodesic distance between nodes $i$ and $j$, calculated approximately using the Haversine formula.

The results for the \texttt{Random pair} and \texttt{Random random} algorithms are displayed in figure~\ref{fig:Portugal_rail} for the steady states obtained with the smallest values of $L$ and $\nu$ values. The smallest $\nu$ tree was generated using the \texttt{Random pair} algorithm, closely followed by \texttt{Random random}. The geometry predicted by the \texttt{Random pair} algorithm is more efficient than the existing railway network. The geometries generated with the adaptive incompressible H-P flow algorithm could be further optimised, particularly regarding the presence of Steiner-type points.


