\section{Conclusions}

We applied a formalism describing adaptive incompressible Hagen-Poiseuille flows in channel networks within $\mathcal{N}$-dimensional Euclidean spaces to identify graphs connecting a finite set of nodes. These graphs can represent communication systems and Steiner tree problems. The networks are embedded in a predefined graph $\mathcal{G}$ obtained through Delaunay triangulation from a $\mathcal{N}$-dimensional point lattice. The area of the Delaunay triangles can be sufficiently small to enable our system to reach a continuous limit. This embedding makes the adaptation process unidimensional along the graph's edges. The procedure is computationally efficient, as multiple copies for various initial conditions can be computed, allowing the selection of suboptimal or optimal subgraph and tree solutions that algebraic methods can further optimise. This approach is particularly advantageous as a practical method for searching for approximate solutions to Steiner tree configurations.

We have applied this adaptive incompressible Hagen-Poiseuille formalism to the design of communication systems or networks of veins in organisms. The solutions obtained are either suboptimal or optimal, minimising the lengths of node connections or the cost of transportation. These solutions emerge within a sufficiently large set of steady-state solutions of the adaptive incompressible Hagen-Poiseuille flows. Furthermore, the suboptimality of the solutions highlights the adaptability and resilience of graph networks against perturbations. The approach to designing communication systems presented here offers a complementary and computationally efficient algorithm to explore and develop adaptive network routing 
\cite{kel}.


