Another effect of the nonlinearities is that they can generate Markovian dissipation terms even if there were none at the outset (see App. \ref{App:GeneratingMarkovianPerturbations}). However, as we show in appendix \ref{App:RelevanceNonMarkovian}, these terms are less relevant than the original non-Markovian dissipation.

{\it Conclusions.--}
We have established the conditions for the stabilization of long-range order in one- and two-dimensional driven $O(N)$ models coupled to non-Markovian baths. Long range order, which is ruled out in equilibrium, is stabilized by the interplay of the conserved angular momenta with the long memory of the bath, decaying as a  power-law in time. If the angular momentum conjugate to the order parameter is not conserved, then the long range order is generally unstable.  


