\section{Introduction}

In many applications of statistical inference, machine learning, pattern recognition, image and video analysis, information-theoretic divergence measures play a key role~\cite{cichocki2011generalized,roy2024robust}. By using these divergence measures to quantify the separation between a family of model distributions and the true data-generating distribution, the minimum divergence estimators have become a popular tool in statistical estimation, due to their simplicity and natural interpretability~\cite{basu2011statistical}. 

Within this realm, certain categories of divergences stand out for their strong robustness and high efficiency. Popular choices include the squared Euclidean distance (also called as $L^2$ distance), the Cressie-Read power divergence family~\cite{cressie1984multinomial} which includes many notable divergences such as the Kullback Leibler (KL) divergence, Pearson’s chi-square, Neyman's chi-square, Hellinger distance, and general chi-square type divergences. Basu et al.~\cite{basu1998robust} further introduced the density power divergence (DPD) family (also called the Beta divergence~\cite{cichocki2010families}), which acts as a bridge between the Kullback Leibler divergence and the $L^2$ distance. Generalizing the above, Ghosh and Basu~\cite{ghosh2017generalized} proposed the super divergence (S-divergence) family, that connects the power divergence family with the density power divergence family. A different generalization starting from the logarithmic density power divergence (LDPD) family was proposed by Maji et al.~\cite{maji2014logarithmic}, resulting in logarithmic super-divergence (LSD) class. This is also closely related to the family of Gamma divergences~\cite{fujisawa2008robust}. Other independent efforts to produce newer classes of statistical divergence measures are also noted in the literature. Starting with the Alpha divergence~\cite{chernoff1952measure}, Cichocki et al.~\cite{cichocki2011generalized} produced a wider class of divergence called the Alpha-Beta (AB) divergence family. In another direction, Jones et al.~\cite{jones2001comparison} proposed a new class of divergence family, which provides a smooth bridge between the DPD family and the LDPD family based on a hyperparameter $\phi$.

These divergence measures and their relationships provide guidance to develop new divergences with interesting properties such as robustness, efficiency, duality, convexity, etc. which are useful in different applications~\cite{cichocki2010families}. In this paper, we demonstrate that all the above mentioned divergences are subclasses of an even wider class of divergence, called ``Generalized Alpha-Beta'' (GAB) divergences. This wider class of divergences also satisfies desirable duality and convexity properties. A similar divergence measure was first conceptualized by Maji~\cite{maji2019contributions}, but the author did not provide any characterization of the corresponding divergence. In fact, it was not even known if the resulting form is a valid statistical divergence, i.e., is nonnegative for all possible argument pairs of the divergence. In this paper, we first provide the characterization of this divergence and develop a necessary and sufficient condition for the resulting form to be a valid statistical divergence. Furthermore, we develop various information-theoretic properties of this general class of divergence and its associated entropy measure.

The rest of the paper is organized as follows. In Section~\ref{sec:prelim}, we introduce some notations and relevant definitions. Following this, in Section~\ref{sec:gsd-alpha-beta}, we provide the definition of Generalized Alpha-Beta (GAB) divergence. Moving on, we develop the necessary and sufficient conditions for the nonnegativity of the GAB divergence, and provide a general framework to construct new statistical divergence measures. We also establish various interesting properties of the resulting divergence. In Section~\ref{sec:gab-entropy}, we introduce the associated family of entropy measures, discuss their properties and link them to different properties of the GAB divergence. Finally, we conclude the paper in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} by discussing the scope of future research extending beyond the current work. For the continuity of the presentation, the proofs of the results presented in the paper are deferred till the Appendix.


\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:prelim}

\subsection{Notations}\label{sec:notation}

Let us first indicate all the notations to be used throughout this paper. Assume that $\Acal$ is a set of alphabets under consideration, which may or may not be finite. We use the symbols $P$ and $Q$ to indicate two generic sub-probability measures both dominated by a common measure $\mu$ defined on the alphabet $\Acal$. Let $p$ and $q$ denote Radon-Nikodym derivatives of $P$ and $Q$ respectively, and be referred to as the sub-density functions. The set of sub-probability distributions is denoted as
\begin{equation*}
    \Omega := \left\{ P: p(a) \geq 0, \forall a \in \Acal, \text{ and } \int_{\Acal} p(a)\dd\mu(a) \leq 1 \right\}.   
\end{equation*}
\noindent When $P$ is a probability distribution, $\int_{\Acal} p(a)d\mu(a)$ equals $1$ and let $\Omega^\ast$ denote the set of $P$s satisfying this. In this case, the corresponding sub-density functions $p$ and $q$ are simply termed as density functions, in accordance to the common nomenclature. If the alphabet set $\Acal$ is finite, then its elements are denoted as $a_1, \dots, a_n$. In this case, we use the shorthand notation $p_i$ to denote $p(a_i)$ for any $i = 1, 2, \dots, n$. The set of sub-probability distributions and probability distributions in this case are respectively denoted as $\Omega_n$ and $\Omega_n^\ast$, with subscripts $n$ to indicate the finiteness of underlying $\Acal$.

For a sub-probability distribution $P$ with sub-density function $p$, we denote its $\alpha$-th norm as 
\begin{equation*}
    \norm{p}_\alpha = \left( \int_{\Acal} p^\alpha(a)\dd\mu(a) \right)^{1/\alpha},    
\end{equation*}
\noindent for any $\alpha \neq 0$, provided that the integral exists and is finite. If the integral does not converge, we assume $\norm{p}_\alpha = \infty$ in this case. Clearly, if $\Acal$ is finite, then such integral always exists and is finite. We shall also indicate the collection of probability distributions with finite $\alpha$-th norm as $L_\alpha(\mu)$, where $\mu$ denotes the dominating measure with respect to which the integral is performed. In the same spirit, we define a $(\alpha,\beta)$-inner product between $P$ (with density $p$) and $Q$ (with density $q$) as
\begin{equation*}
    \inner{p, q}_{\alpha,\beta} = \int_{\Acal} p^\alpha(a) q^\beta(a) \dd\mu(a),   
\end{equation*}
\noindent for all $\alpha,\beta \in \R$. Note that this is not a proper inner product in the mathematical sense since it does not satisfy the commutative property. 

For any $\alpha \neq 0$ and a sub-probability distribution $P$, let $P^\alpha$ denote the sub-probability distribution corresponding to the sub-density function $p^\alpha$, provided that $\int_{\Acal} p^\alpha(a) d\mu(a) \leq 1$. These are also denoted as the unnormalized $\alpha$-zoomed versions of the original probability distribution $P$~\cite{cichocki2010families}. On the other hand, let $P^{[\alpha]}$ and $p^{[\alpha]}$ denote the normalized $\alpha$-zoomed versions of the distribution and the density, from the original distribution $P$, i.e., $p^{[\alpha]} = p^\alpha/\norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha$.

We also use the notation $C^k([a, b])$ to denote the class of all functions on $[a, b]$ which are $k$-times differentiable and the $k$-th derivative is continuous. Extending this notation, we denote the set of all continuous functions on $[a, b]$ as $C^0([a, b])$. The notation $\ind{A}$ denotes the indicator of the set $A$, i.e., $\ind{A}(x) = 1$ if $x \in A$ and $0$ otherwise.

\subsection{Alpha-Beta Divergence}\label{sec:alpha-beta}

Given two probability distributions $P$ and $Q$ with densities $p$ and $q$ respectively, Cichocki et al.~\cite{cichocki2011generalized} define the Alpha-Beta (AB) divergence between them as
\begin{equation}
    d_{AB}^{(\alpha,\beta)}(P, Q)
    = \dfrac{1}{\alpha\beta}\left( \lambda_\alpha \norm{p}_{\alpha+\beta}^{\alpha+\beta} + \lambda_\beta \norm{q}_{\alpha+\beta}^{\alpha+\beta} - \inner{p,q}_{\alpha,\beta} \right)
    \label{eqn:defn-ab-div} 
\end{equation}
\noindent where $\lambda_\alpha = \alpha/(\alpha+\beta)$ and $\lambda_\beta = (1 - \lambda_\alpha)$, if $\alpha\beta(\alpha+\beta)\neq 0$. However, if $\alpha\beta(\alpha+\beta) = 0$, the form of AB divergence is defined as the corresponding limits of the form given in~\eqref{eqn:defn-ab-div}, which turns out to be,
\begin{equation}
    d_{AB}^{(\alpha,\beta)}(P, Q)
    = \begin{cases} 
        \dfrac{\int p^\alpha \ln\left( p^\alpha/q^\alpha \right) - \norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha + \norm{q}_\alpha^\alpha }{\alpha^2},  
        & \text{if } \alpha \neq 0, \beta = 0, \\
        \dfrac{\int q^\beta \ln\left( q^\beta/p^\beta \right) - \norm{q}_\beta^\beta + \norm{p}_\beta^\beta}{\beta^2},  
        & \text{if } \alpha = 0, \beta \neq 0, \\
        \dfrac{\int \ln\left(q^\alpha / p^\alpha\right) + \int p^\alpha/q^\alpha - 1}{\alpha^2},  
        & \text{if } \alpha = -\beta \neq 0, \\
        \dfrac{1}{2}\int (\ln p - \ln q)^2,  
        & \text{if } \alpha = \beta = 0.
    \end{cases}
    \label{eqn:defn-ab-div-zero}
\end{equation}
\noindent All the integrals above are performed over the alphabet set $\Acal$ and with respect to the dominating measure $\mu$, which is suppressed in the notation. If $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are both nonzero, and $(\alpha + \beta) = (1 + \tau)$ for some $\tau \geq 0$, then a simple reorganization of the terms of~\eqref{eqn:defn-ab-div} makes the AB divergence equivalent to the S-divergence introduced by Ghosh et al.~\cite{ghosh2017generalized}; as observed in the relation,
\begin{align*}
    \alpha(1+\tau-\alpha)d_{AB}^{(\alpha,1+\tau - \alpha)}(P,Q)
    & = -\inner{p,q}_{\alpha,1+\tau-\alpha} + \frac{\alpha}{1+\tau}\norm{p}_{1+\tau}^{1+\tau} + \frac{1+\tau-\alpha}{1+\tau}\norm{q}_{1+\tau}^{1+\tau}\\
    & = \dfrac{\alpha(1+\tau-\alpha)}{(1+\tau)} d_{S}^{(\tau, (\tau - \alpha)/(1-\tau) )}(P,Q).
\end{align*}
\noindent In another direction, taking $\beta = (1 - \alpha)$ reduces the AB divergence to the Alpha divergence family~\cite{chernoff1952measure} (also known as the Cressie Read power divergence family~\cite{cressie1984multinomial}), 
\begin{equation}
    d_A^{(\alpha)}(P, Q)
    = \begin{cases}
        \dfrac{1}{1-\alpha} \norm{p}_1 + \dfrac{1}{\alpha}\norm{q}_1 - \dfrac{\inner{p,q}_{\alpha,1-\alpha}}{\alpha(1-\alpha)}, & \text{if } \alpha \notin \{ 0, 1 \},\\
        \int p\ln(p/q) - \norm{p}_1 + \norm{q}_1, & \text{if } \alpha = 1,\\
        \int q\ln(q/p) - \norm{q}_1 + \norm{p}_1, & \text{if } \alpha = 0,
    \end{cases}
    \label{eqn:defn-a-div}
\end{equation}
\noindent which is also a special case of the S-divergence with $\tau = 0$. On the other hand, when $\alpha = 1$, it reduces to the Beta divergence
\begin{equation}
    d_B^{(\beta)}(P, Q)
    = \begin{cases}
        \dfrac{\frac{1}{\beta}\norm{p}_{1+\beta}^{1+\beta} - \frac{1+\beta}{\beta}\inner{p, q}_{1,\beta} + \norm{q}_{1+\beta}^{1+\beta}}{1+\beta},  & \text{if } \beta \notin \{ -1, 0 \}\\
        \int p\ln(p/q) - \norm{p}_1 + \norm{q}_1, & \text{if } \beta = 0,\\
        \int \ln(q/p) + \int p/q - 1, & \text{if } \beta = (-1),
    \end{cases}
    \label{eqn:defn-b-div}
\end{equation}
\noindent The class of Beta divergence contains a scaled version of density power divergence family~\cite{basu1998robust} as a special case with $\beta > 0$.

\subsection{Logarithmic Alpha-Beta Divergence}\label{sec:lab-div-defn}

Generalizing the Alpha-Beta (AB) divergence with a nonlinear transformation like logarithm, Cichocki et al.~\cite{cichocki2011generalized} indicated a new family of divergences 
\begin{equation}
    d_{AC}^{(\alpha,\beta)}(P, Q)
    = \dfrac{1}{\beta(\alpha+\beta)} \ln\left( \norm{p}_{\alpha+\beta}^{\alpha+\beta} \right)
    + \dfrac{1}{\alpha(\alpha+\beta)} \ln\left( \norm{q}_{\alpha+\beta}^{\alpha+\beta} \right)
    - \dfrac{1}{\alpha\beta} \ln\left( \inner{p,q}_{\alpha,\beta} \right),
    \label{eqn:defn-ac-div}
\end{equation}
\noindent for $\alpha \beta (\alpha+\beta) \neq 0$. This divergence closely relates to the Gamma divergence family~\cite{cichocki2010families}. In a separate attempt, Maji et al.~\cite{maji2014logarithmic,maji2016logarithmic} also considered the same logarithmic transformation starting with the S-divergence (SD) family, which resulted in the logarithmic S-divergence (LSD) family.


\section{Generalized Alpha-Beta divergence}\label{sec:gsd-alpha-beta}

\subsection{Definition}\label{sec:gab-defn}

When $\alpha, \beta, (\alpha+\beta) \neq 0$, both the statistical divergence measures given in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:defn-ab-div} and Eq.~\eqref{eqn:defn-ac-div} have a similar form, two terms containing the $(\alpha+\beta)$-th norm of the densities $p$ and $q$, and a single term containing the $(\alpha,\beta)$-inner product between $p$ and $q$. Generalizing this form with an arbitrary nonlinear transformation, one may obtain a newer class of divergence measures characterized by the hyperparameters $\alpha, \beta$ and the transformation $\psi$. Some assumptions are necessary for this transformation to produce a well-defined statistical divergence measure, and these conditions are described later in detail in Section~\ref{sec:gab-char}. Let, $\psi: [0, \infty] \rightarrow \R$ be such a suitable transformation. Then, we define the Generalized Alpha-Beta (GAB) divergence between any two sub-probability distributions $P$ and $Q$ as
\begin{equation}
    d_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}(P, Q)
    = \dfrac{1}{\beta(\alpha+\beta)} \psi\left( \norm{p}_{\alpha+\beta}^{\alpha+\beta} \right)
    + \dfrac{1}{\alpha(\alpha+\beta)} \psi\left( \norm{q}_{\alpha+\beta}^{\alpha+\beta} \right)
    - \dfrac{1}{\alpha\beta} \psi\left( \inner{p,q}_{\alpha,\beta} \right),
    \label{eqn:defn-gab-div}
\end{equation}
\noindent when $\alpha, \beta, (\alpha+\beta)$ are all nonzero. If $\psi \in C^1([0,\infty))$, then one can define the form of Generalized Alpha-Beta divergence for the edge cases, i.e., when $\alpha = 0, \beta = 0$ or $\alpha + \beta = 0$ by taking the corresponding limits of the GAB divergence,
\begin{equation}
    d_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}(P, Q)
    = \begin{cases}
        \frac{\psi'(\norm{p}_{\alpha}^{\alpha}) d^\ast(P^\alpha,Q^\alpha) - \psi(\norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha) + \psi(\norm{q}_\alpha^\alpha)}{\alpha^2}, & \text{if } \alpha \neq 0, \beta = 0,\\
        \frac{\psi'(\norm{q}_{\beta}^{\beta}) d^\ast(Q^\beta, P^\beta) - \psi(\norm{q}_\beta^\beta) + \psi(\norm{p}_\beta^\beta)}{\beta^2}, & \text{if } \alpha = 0, \beta \neq 0,\\
        \frac{ \psi'(1) \int \ln(q^\alpha/p^\alpha) + \psi\left( \norm{p/q}_\alpha^{\alpha} \right) - \psi(1) }{\alpha^2}, & \text{if } \alpha = -\beta \neq 0,\\
        \frac{\psi'(1)}{2}\int (\ln p - \ln q)^2, & \text{if } \alpha = \beta = 0.
    \end{cases}
    \label{eqn:defn-gab-div-edge}
\end{equation}
\noindent where
\begin{equation}
    d^\ast(P, Q) = \int p\ln \left( p/q \right),
    \label{eqn:d-kl-1}
\end{equation}
\noindent denotes the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence between $P$ and $Q$. In Eq.~\eqref{eqn:defn-gab-div-edge}, the quantity $d^\ast(P^\alpha, Q^\alpha)$ thus indicates the KL divergence between the unnormalized $\alpha$-zoomed versions of $P$ and $Q$.

A version of this generalized divergence measure had been conceptualized by Maji~\cite{maji2019contributions} but without any discussions on the valid choices for the class of $\psi$-functions. Clearly, with $\psi(x) = x$, it reduces to the usual AB divergence while $\psi(x) = \ln(x)$ leads to the logarithmic Alpha-Beta divergence. Hence, the proposed Generalized Alpha-Beta (GAB) divergence is a superclass containing many important types of divergences beyond these two specific choices. Some of these important special cases are indicated in Table~\ref{tab:gab-divergence-families}.

