\subsection{Characterization of the GAB divergence}\label{sec:gab-char}

The forms of the GAB divergence as given in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:defn-gab-div} do not necessarily mean that the given form is a valid statistical divergence measure for all choices of generating function $\psi$. A statistical divergence must satisfy the nonnegativity property for all choices of its arguments. Therefore, we wish to find out the necessary and sufficient conditions on the generating function $\psi$ for which, 
\begin{equation*}
    d_{GAB}^{(\alpha, \beta),\psi}(P, Q) \geq 0,
\end{equation*}
\noindent for all sub-probability distributions $P$ and $Q$. Also, it must be the case that if $P = Q$, then the above inequality must hold with equality.

If both $\alpha, \beta > 0$, then it is easy to verify by an application of H\"{o}lder's inequality and Jensen's inequality that if $\psi$ is monotonically increasing and convex, the form given in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:defn-gab-div} is nonnegative for all pairs of $P$ and $Q$. However, this characterization does not encompass the entire class of generating functions $\psi$ for which the resulting GAB divergence is a valid divergence. In particular, the family of logarithmic super-divergences (LSD) or the logarithmic Alpha-Beta divergence which are known to be valid divergence measures~\cite{ghosh2017generalized}, it has a corresponding generating function $\ln(x)$ which is not convex.

To completely characterize this class of valid generating functions, we follow the footsteps of Ray et al.~\cite{ray2023functional}. In the end, our results turn out to be a generalization of the results of Ray et al.~\cite{ray2023functional} presented in the context of Functional Density Power Divergence (FDPD) class, as we cover a significantly larger class of divergence measures with general choices of $\alpha$ and $\beta$. The results for the FDPD will then follow from our general results with a replacement of $\alpha = 1$. Also, because the forms of GAB as in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:defn-gab-div-edge} are only valid when the generating function $\psi$ is differentiable with a continuous first-order derivative, we shall restrict our attention only to the class of functions $C^1([0,\infty))$ throughout this paper. Although it is possible to have a statistically valid divergence with not-so-smooth $\psi$ functions (as in the case of FDPD~\cite{ray2023functional}), the interesting properties of the resulting minimum divergence estimators emerge only for sufficiently smooth choices of $\psi$ functions (e.g. $\psi(x) = x, \psi(x) = \ln(x), \psi(x) = x^\gamma/\gamma$, etc.).

A key ingredient of our results is the usage of the function $\Psi(x) := \psi(e^x)$, on which we will apply monotonicity and convexity conditions. To develop the necessary and sufficient conditions for the GAB divergence to be a valid divergence measure, we shall consider three separate cases, 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\alpha + \beta = 1$.
    \item $\alpha\beta \neq 0$ and $\alpha+\beta \notin \{0, 1\}$.
    \item Other limiting cases where either $\alpha = 0$ or $\beta = 0$.
\end{enumerate}

First, we consider the special case $\alpha +\beta = 1$. In this case, the GAB divergence reduces to
\begin{equation*}
    d_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}(P, Q)
    = \begin{cases}
        \frac{1}{\alpha(1-\alpha)}\left( \psi(1) - \psi(\inner{p,q}_{\alpha, 1-\alpha}) \right), & \text{if } \alpha \notin \{ 0, 1 \},\\
        \psi'(1) d^\ast(P, Q), & \text{if } \alpha = 1, \beta = 0,\\
        \psi'(1) d^\ast(Q, P), & \text{if } \alpha = 0, \beta = 1.
    \end{cases}
\end{equation*}
\noindent where $d^\ast$ is as given in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:d-kl-1}. For the form for the limiting cases, it clearly follows that it is a valid divergence if and only if $\psi'(1) > 0$, i.e., $\psi$ is strictly increasing at $1$. We shall show that this is a necessary and sufficient condition for this special case of the GAB divergence.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:gab-div-alpha-plus-beta-1}
    For $\alpha + \beta = 1$, the Generalized Alpha-Beta divergence is nonnegative if and only if the corresponding generating function $\psi$ satisfies the following:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item When either $\alpha = 1$ or $\beta = 1$, $\psi$ is increasing at $1$.
        \item When $\alpha \in (0, 1)$, then $\psi$ is increasing in a neighbourhood of $1$ of the form $(1-\epsilon, 1]$ for some $\epsilon > 0$.
        \item When $\alpha \notin (0, 1)$, then $\psi$ is increasing in a neighbourhood of $1$ of the form $[1, 1+\epsilon)$ for some $\epsilon > 0$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

Now that we have established a characterization for the special case $\alpha + \beta = 1$ of the GAB divergence, we turn our attention to the case when $\alpha + \beta \neq 1$. Additionally, suppose that $\alpha, \beta$ and $(\alpha+\beta)$ are all nonzero as well. The following proposition establishes the necessary condition on $\psi(\cdot)$ to produce to a valid GAB divergence in this case.

\begin{prop}\label{lem:gab-sufficient}
    Define $\Psi(x) := \psi(e^x)$ for all $x \in [0, \infty)$. If $\Psi$ is strictly increasing and convex, then the GAB generated by $\psi$ is nonnegative for all choices of $\alpha, \beta, (\alpha+\beta) \neq 0$. 
\end{prop}

When $\alpha = 1$ and $\beta > 0$, then Proposition~\ref{lem:gab-sufficient} reduces to the sufficient condition for the $\psi$ function of the FDPD, as in the Proposition 4.1 of Ray et al.~\cite{ray2023functional}. Thus, Proposition~\ref{lem:gab-sufficient} is a generalization of this result to the family of generalized Alpha-Beta divergences. Similar to the case of FDPD, it turns out that the condition of the geometric convexity and monotonicity of the $\psi$ function is also a necessary condition for the GAB divergence to be a valid statistical divergence.

\begin{prop}\label{lem:gab-necessary}
    If the GAB divergence with a generating function $\psi \in C^1([0, \infty))$ is nonnegative, then $\Psi(x) := \psi(e^x)$ must be strictly increasing and convex, for any choice of $\alpha, \beta, (\alpha+\beta) \neq 0$.
\end{prop}

Therefore, combining the Propositions~\ref{lem:gab-sufficient} and~\ref{lem:gab-necessary}, we now have a necessary and sufficient condition for the proposed GAB divergence to be a valid divergence for all the non-limiting cases. This is formulated as a theorem below. 

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:gab-div-nec-suff}
    Let $\psi \in C^1([0, \infty))$ and define $\Psi(x) := \psi(e^x)$ for all $x \in \R$. Suppose $\alpha, \beta \neq 0$ and $(\alpha+\beta) \notin \{0, 1\}$. Then, the GAB divergence generated by the function $\psi$ is always nonnegative if and only if $\Psi$ is strictly increasing and convex.
\end{theorem}

Before we proceed further to establish similar necessary and sufficient conditions for the nonnegativity of the GAB divergence for $\alpha = 0$ or $\beta = 0$ cases, we rewrite the form of GAB divergence to show its connection to the popular Kullback Leibler (KL) divergence. 
\begin{align*}
    \int p^\alpha\ln(p^\alpha/q^\alpha)
    & = \norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha \int p^{[\alpha]}\ln\left(p^{[\alpha]} \norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha / q^{[\alpha]} \norm{q}_\alpha^\alpha \right)\\
    & = \norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha \left[ d^\ast(P^{[\alpha]}, Q^{[\alpha]}) + \ln(\norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha) - \ln(\norm{q}_\alpha^\alpha) \right],
\end{align*}
\noindent where $d^\ast(P^{[\alpha]}, Q^{[\alpha]})$ is the KL divergence between the normalized $\alpha$-zoomed versions of $P$ and $Q$, see Eq.~\eqref{eqn:d-kl-1}. Therefore, we can rewrite the GAB divergence for $\alpha \neq 0, \beta = 0$ case as 
\begin{equation}
    \alpha^2 d_{GAB}^{(\alpha,0),\psi}(P, Q)
    = \psi'(\norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha) \norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha \left( d^\ast(P^{[\alpha]}, Q^{[\alpha]}) + \ln(\norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha) - \ln(\norm{q}_\alpha^\alpha) \right)
    - \psi(\norm{p}_\alpha^\alpha) + \psi(\norm{q}_\alpha^\alpha).
    \label{eqn:gab-to-kl-bzero}
\end{equation}
\noindent Similarly, for $\alpha = 0, \beta \neq 0$ case, we get
\begin{equation}
    \beta^2 d_{GAB}^{(0,\beta),\psi}(P, Q)
    = \psi'(\norm{q}_\beta^\beta) \norm{q}_\beta^\beta \left( d^\ast(Q^{[\beta]}, P^{[\beta]}) + \ln(\norm{q}_\beta^\beta) - \ln(\norm{p}_\beta^\beta) \right)
    - \psi(\norm{q}_\beta^\beta) + \psi(\norm{p}_\beta^\beta).
    \label{eqn:gab-to-kl-azero}
\end{equation}
\noindent This connection demonstrates that the GAB divergence for $\alpha = 0, \beta \neq 0$ or $\alpha \neq 0, \beta = 0$ can be visualized as a localized affine transformation of the KL divergence between the zoomed versions of the corresponding densities. In the first case if $\beta > 0$, the corresponding tails of the densities are downweighted. This results in higher robustness than the usual minimum KL-divergence estimator, i.e., the maximum likelihood estimator (MLE). 

In the following proposition, we present sufficient conditions for the GAB divergence to be nonnegative for these two limiting cases, which follows easily from Eq.~\eqref{eqn:gab-to-kl-bzero}-\eqref{eqn:gab-to-kl-azero}.

\begin{prop}\label{lem:gab-sufficient-azero}
    Define $\Psi(x) := \psi(e^x)$ for all $x \in \R$. If $\Psi$ is strictly increasing and is convex for all $x \in \R$, then the GAB divergence generated by $\psi$ is nonnegative when either $\alpha \neq 0, \beta = 0$ or $\alpha = 0, \beta \neq 0$.
\end{prop}

As the cases $\alpha = 1, \beta = 0$ or $\alpha = 0, \beta = 1$ are already discussed in Theorem~\ref{thm:gab-div-alpha-plus-beta-1}, while demonstrating the necessary conditions, we disregard these two specific cases.

\begin{prop}\label{lem:gab-necessary-azero}
    Suppose either $\alpha \notin \{0, 1\}, \beta = 0$ or $\alpha = 0, \beta \notin \{0, 1\}$ holds. If the GAB divergence with generating function $\psi \in C^1(\R)$ is nonnegative, then $\Psi(x) := \psi(e^x)$ must be strictly increasing and convex.
\end{prop}

Complementing Theorem~\ref{thm:gab-div-nec-suff}, a combination of the Propositions~\ref{lem:gab-sufficient-azero} and~\ref{lem:gab-necessary-azero} leads to the necessary and sufficient condition for the GAB divergence to be a valid measure of statistical divergence, for the limiting cases when either $\alpha = 0$ or $\beta = 0$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:gab-div-nec-suff-azero}
    Let $\psi \in C^1([0, \infty))$ and define $\Psi(x) := \psi(e^x)$ for all $x \in \R$. Suppose exactly one of $\alpha$ and $\beta$ is equal to $0$, and $(\alpha+\beta) \neq 1$. Then, the GAB divergence generated by the function $\psi$ is always nonnegative if and only if $\Psi$ is strictly increasing and convex.
\end{theorem}

Theorems~\ref{thm:gab-div-alpha-plus-beta-1}, \ref{thm:gab-div-nec-suff} and \ref{thm:gab-div-nec-suff-azero} together completely characterize the necessary and sufficient conditions for the proposed Generalized Alpha-Beta (GAB) divergence to be a valid statistical divergence measure. Except the case $\alpha + \beta = 1$, the condition is the increasing and convexity property of the $\Psi$ function. In the special case when $\alpha + \beta = 1$, it requires a much weaker condition, only locally increasing behaviour of $\Psi$ at $x = 1$ suffices.


\subsection{Construction of new divergences}\label{sec:gab-construct}

The results presented in Section~\ref{sec:gab-char} allow us to construct new divergence measures by using any increasing and convex function as $\Psi$. We have already seen the special cases corresponding to $\Psi(x) = e^{cx}$ or $\Psi(x) = cx$ for some $c > 0$, which results in the AB divergence and logarithmic AB divergence respectively. Beyond this, we may consider $\Psi(x) = \ln(1 + e^{cx})$ for some $c > 0$, or more generally $\Psi(x) = \ln(c_1 + e^{c_2x})$ for any $c_1, c_2 > 0$. Note that, the corresponding generating function in this case becomes $\psi(x) = \ln(c_1 + x^{c_2})$, which results in a generalization of the bridge divergence constructed by Kuchibhotla et al.~\cite{kuchibhotla2019statistical}, which is also independently proposed by Gayen et al.~\cite{gayen2024unified}.

One may also consider any piecewise linear function such that it is continuous and the slopes of each of the segment is increasing. Namely, take 
\begin{equation*}
    \Psi(x) = \begin{cases}
        a_1 x + b_1 & \text{ if } x \in (-\infty, c_1],\\
        a_2 x + b_2 & \text{ if } x \in (c_1, c_2],\\
        \dots, \\
        a_k x + b_k & \text{ if } x \in (c_{k-1}, c_k],\\
        a_{k+1}x + b_{k+1} & \text{ if } x \in (c_k, \infty)
    \end{cases}
\end{equation*}
\noindent such that $a_1 < a_2 < \dots < a_{k+1}$ and $a_ic_i + b_i = a_{i+1}c_i + b_{i+1}$ for all $i = 1, \dots, k$ to ensure continuity. Note that, although this $\Psi$ function lies in $C^0([0,\infty))$ instead of $C^1([0, \infty))$, its increasing and convex nature ensures that the resulting GAB divergence is nonnegative, expect for the edge cases with $\alpha\beta(\alpha+\beta) = 0$; see Proposition~\ref{lem:gab-sufficient}. The corresponding generating function $\psi(x)$ is taken to be $\Psi(\ln(x))$. One may regard this choice as an approximation of a smooth generating function.

% \sr{Will the divergence be continuous through the limit of these smooth approximations? If yes, then these are like simple functions in measure theory.}

Additionally, if we have two characteristic functions $\psi_1$ and $\psi_2$ corresponding to two different GAB divergence measures, we can also combine them to construct new classes of GAB divergence.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item By the necessary conditions given in Section~\ref{sec:gab-char}, we know that the corresponding $\Psi_1$ and $\Psi_2$ will be increasing and convex functions. Clearly, any linear combination of them with positive coefficients will also be increasing and convex. This means, for any $c_1, c_2 > 0$, $\psi := c_1 \psi_1 + c_2 \psi_2$ leads to a valid GAB divergence. One can also establish this by direct verification based on the form~\eqref{eqn:defn-gab-div}.
    \item A slightly nontrivial way to combine $\Psi_1$ and $\Psi_2$ is to consider their composition. Since the composition $\Psi_1 \circ \Psi_2$ is also increasing and convex, it follows that $\psi(x) := \Psi_1(\psi_2(x))$ is a valid characterizing function.
\end{enumerate}

A general way to build a valid characterizing function is to begin with a density function $f(x)$, and take $\Psi(x) = \int_{-\infty}^x F(t)dt$ where $F(x)$ is the corresponding cumulative distribution function. Note that, $\Psi(\cdot)$ is increasing and convex by construction. With this choice, we take the characterizing function $\psi(x) = \Psi(\ln(x)) = \int_{-\infty}^{\ln(x)} F(t)dt$. For example, for the Dirac delta distribution at $x = 0$, the resulting $\psi$ becomes $\ln(x)$, leading to the logarithmic AB divergence. To illustrate more examples, starting with the exponential distribution, we obtain
\begin{equation*}
    \psi_{\text{Exp}}(x)
    = \begin{cases}
        0 & \text{ if } x < 1\\
        1/x + \ln(x) - 1 & \text{ if } x \geq 1
    \end{cases},
\end{equation*}
\noindent and considering the normal distribution, one obtains
\begin{align*}
    \psi_{\text{Normal}}(x)
    & = \int_{-\infty}^{\ln(x)} \Phi(t)dt\\
    & = \int_{-\infty}^{\ln(x)} \frac{\ln(x) - u}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-u^2/2}du\\
    & = \ln(x) \Phi(\ln(x)) + \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-\ln^2(x)/2},
\end{align*}
\noindent where $\Phi(t)$ is the standard normal cumulative distribution function. We illustrate a few interesting choices of the generating function $\psi$ in Figure~\ref{fig:gab-div-psi-functions}. 

