\subsection{Properties of GABE}\label{sec:gabe-properties}

It can be easily seen from the definition in~\eqref{eqn:gab-entropy} that the GABE satisfies the following properties.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Continuity:} For a finite alphabet $\mathcal{A}$, the GAB entropy is continuous in the $p_i = p(a_i)$s for all $i = 1, 2, \dots, \vert \Acal\vert$.
    \item \textbf{Symmetricity:} $\varepsilon_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}(P)$ is a symmetric function of $\{ p_1, p_2, \dots p_{\vert \Acal\vert} \}$.
    \item \textbf{Decisivity:} For any degenerate distribution $\delta_x$ at $x \in \mathcal{A}$, $\varepsilon_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}(\delta_x) = 0$.
    \item \textbf{Expandibility:} For any sub-probability distribution $P := (p_1, \dots, p_n) \in \Omega_n$, we have 
    \begin{equation*}
        \varepsilon_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}(P) = \varepsilon_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}(P^\ast),        
    \end{equation*}
    \noindent where $P^\ast := (p_1, \dots, p_n, 0) \in \Omega_{n+1}$ is another sub-probability distribution over a new alphabet set having $(n+1)$ elements.
    \item \textbf{Maximum value:} For a finite alphabet $\Acal$ with $\vert \Acal\vert = n$, the maximum value of the proposed entropy measure is $\psi(n^{\alpha+\beta-1})/\alpha(\alpha+\beta)$. This is a simple consequence of the nonnegativity of the GAB divergence and the construction of the GABE as given in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:gab-entropy}.
\end{enumerate}

A desirable property of an entropy measure is the additivity property. When this property holds, then the joint entropy of two systems governed by independent probability distributions $P$ and $Q$ becomes same as the sum of their individual entropy measures. Shanon's entropy and the logarithmic norm entropy both satisfy this additivity property. In the following theorem, we show that the generalized alpha-beta entropy also satisfies such a property provided the generating $\psi$-function satisfies a generalized additivity property.

\begin{prop}\label{thm:gabe-subaddivity}
    Let $P \in \Omega_n, Q \in \Omega_m$ for some $n, m \in \Z$. Let $R \equiv P \ast Q = (p_iq_j)_{i=1,j=1}^{n,m}$ denote their independent combination such that $\R \in \Omega_{nm}$. Suppose further that for any $x, y > 0$, the function $\psi$ satisfy
    \begin{equation}
        \psi(xy) = g(\psi(x)) + h(\psi(y)),
        \label{eqn:psi-genealized-mult-sum}
    \end{equation}
    \noindent for some function $g$ and $h$. Then the GAB entropy satisfies a generalized Shannon-additivity property, i.e.,
    \begin{equation*}
        \varepsilon_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}(r) = \varepsilon_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),g \circ \psi}(p) + \varepsilon_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),h \circ \psi}(q),
    \end{equation*}
    \noindent where the notation $f \circ g$ denotes the composition of two functions $f$ and $g$.
\end{prop}

Another important property of any entropy measure is its concavity which allows one to apply the maximum entropy principle. In the following, we derive the exact set of conditions under which the concavity of the GAB entropy follows. A similar set of conditions for the logarithmic norm entropy is present in Ghosh and Basu~\cite{ghosh2021scale}. Before proceeding with the main result, we start with a short lemma that characterizes the behaviour of $\psi(\norm{p}_a^a)$ as a function of the density $p$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:convex-psi}
    The quantity $\psi(\norm{p}_a^a)$ is convex in $P \in \Omega$ if any of the following holds
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $a < 0$ and $\Psi(x) := \psi(e^x)$ is nondecreasing and convex.
        \item $a > 0$ such that $\ln(\norm{p}_a)$ is convex in $p \in \Omega$ and $\Psi(x) := \psi(e^x)$ is nondecreasing and convex.
        \item $a > 1$ and $\psi(\cdot)$ is nondecreasing and convex.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:gab-entropy-concave}
    The GAB entropy $\varepsilon_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}$ is concave on $\Omega$ if any of the following conditions hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\alpha < 0, \beta > 0$ and $(\alpha+\beta) \in (0, 1) \cup (1, \infty)$ such that $\ln(\norm{p}_{\alpha+\beta})$ is convex for $P \in \Omega$.
        \item $\alpha < 0, \beta > 0, (\alpha + \beta) > 1$ and $\psi(\cdot)$ is convex.
        \item $\alpha > 0, \beta < 0, (\alpha+\beta) < 0$ such that $\ln(\norm{p}_\alpha)$ is convex for $P \in \Omega$.
        \item $\alpha > 1, \beta < 0, (\alpha+\beta) < 0$ and $\psi(\cdot)$ is convex.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}


A consequence of Theorem~\ref{thm:gab-entropy-concave} is that under the same conditions as in Theorem~\ref{thm:gab-entropy-concave}, the GAB divergence is convex in its first argument. This follows directly from the relationship that $\varepsilon_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}(P) = -d_{GAB}^{(\alpha,\beta),\psi}(P, Q_0) + h(Q_0)$ where $Q_0$ is the fixed uniform measure on the alphabet $\mathcal{A}$ and $h(Q_0)$ is a quantity depending only on $Q_0$. On the other hand, because of the duality result~\eqref{eqn:gab-symmetry}, exchanging the role of $\alpha$ and $\beta$ in the conditions presented in Thereom~\ref{thm:gab-entropy-concave}, we obtain a set of sufficient conditions under which the GAB divergence is convex in its second argument.

In the limiting case when $\alpha = 0$, the GAB divergence is convex in its first argument if either $\beta > 0$ and $\ln(\norm{p}_\beta)$ is convex in $p$, or $\beta > 1$ and $\psi(\cdot)$ is convex. To see this, note that due to the form of the divergence given in~\eqref{eqn:defn-gab-div-edge}, it is enough to show that the quantities $-\beta\int q^\beta \ln(p)$ and $\psi(\norm{p}_\beta^\beta)$ are convex in $p$. Clearly, the first function is convex for any $\beta > 0$ due to the concavity of the $\ln(\cdot)$ function. The convexity of the second function follows directly from Lemma~\ref{lem:convex-psi}. 

