\subsection{Maximum Entropy Principle}\label{eqn:maxent-distn}

The maximum entropy principle is a fundamental concept having various applications in statistics, information theory and statistical physics. While various classical literature investigates the maximum entropy distribution under different linear constraints (which translates to various moment-type conditions in statistics), modern literature in information theory generalizes the constraints to consider normalized $\beta$-expectations~\cite{ghosh2021scale}. Here, we consider the constraints for a probability distribution $P \in \Omega_n$ over a finite alphabet set $\Acal$ given by
\begin{equation}
    \dfrac{\sum_{i=1}^n g_r(a_i)p_i^\alpha }{\sum_{i=1}^n p_i^\alpha} = G_r,
    \label{eqn:maxent-constraints}    
\end{equation}
\noindent for $r = 1, 2, \dots, m$, where $g_1, g_2, \dots, g_m: \Acal \rightarrow \R$ are $m$ given functions on $\Acal$ and $G_1, G_2, \dots, G_m$ are given fixed constants. In applications of statistical physics, these constraints in~\eqref{eqn:maxent-constraints} may indicate different physical and chemical properties of particles under a controlled environment in a closed system. The maximum entropy principle, in this case, tells us that the underlying system would aim to maximize its entropy, and the maximizing probability distribution can characterize the behaviour (e.g. position, velocity) of the particles in the closed system~\cite{jaynes1957information,kapur1992entropy}.

Before presenting the result pertaining to the maximum entropy distribution, we define two key notations as follows. For a probability distribution $P \in \Omega_n$, we denote
\begin{align}
    c_1(p) & := \frac{\alpha+\beta}{\alpha} \left[ \ln\left( \norm{p}_{\frac{\alpha+\beta}{\alpha}} \right) - \ln\left( \norm{p}_{1/\alpha} \right) \right],\label{eqn:c1p-defn}\\
    c_2(p) & := -\ln(\norm{p}_{1/\alpha}).\label{eqn:c2p-defn}    
\end{align}

In the following, we now present the result that describes the probability distribution that maximizes GABE subject to the constraints given in~\eqref{eqn:maxent-constraints}. In view of~\eqref{eqn:maxent-constraints}, it is only interesting to consider the case $\alpha \neq 0$. Additionally, since $\alpha + \beta = 1$ case always lead to the power divergence, as illustrated in Theorem~\ref{thm:gab-div-alpha-plus-beta-1}, we additionally restrict our attention to the case when $\alpha + \beta \neq 1$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:maxent-dist}
    Let $\alpha \neq 0, (\alpha+\beta) \neq 1$. For a finite alphabet set $\Acal$ with $\vert \Acal\vert = n$, the probability distribution $P = (p_1, \dots, p_n) \in \Omega_n$ which maximizes the generalized Alpha-Beta entropy as in~\eqref{eqn:gab-entropy} with a valid characteristic function $\psi \in C^2([0,\infty))$ subject to the constraints~\eqref{eqn:maxent-constraints} is given by $Q^{[1/\alpha]}$, the $1/\alpha$-zoomed version of some probability distribution $Q = (q_1, \dots, q_n)$ satisfying the following conditions.

    If $\beta(\alpha+\beta) \neq 0$, then $Q$ satisfies the following fixed-point proportionality relationship,
    \begin{equation}
        q_i \propto \left[ \frac{\Psi'(c_1(q)) - \Psi'(c_2(q))}{e^{-c_2(q)/\alpha}} + \alpha\beta \sum_{r=1}^m \frac{\lambda_r g_r(a_i)}{q_i^{1/\alpha-1}} \right]^{\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta-1}},
        \label{eqn:maxent-dist-type-1}       
    \end{equation}
    \noindent where $\Psi(x) := \psi(e^x)$, as defined before and $c_1(q)$ and $c_2(q)$ are as defined in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:c1p-defn} and~\eqref{eqn:c2p-defn} respectively.

    If $\beta = 0, (\alpha+\beta) \neq 0$, then $Q$ satisfies the following fixed-point proportionality relationship,
    \begin{equation}
        q_i \propto \exp\left[ \dfrac{\alpha^2}{\Psi'(c_2(q))} \left\{ \sum_{r=1}^m \lambda_r g_r(a_i) + \frac{\Psi''(c_2(q))}{e^{-c_2(q)/\alpha}} \left( \sum_{i=1}^n q_i\ln(q_i) + c_2(q) \right) q_i^{\frac{1}{\alpha}-1} \right\} \right].
        \label{eqn:maxent-dist-type-2}
    \end{equation}

    If $\beta \neq 0$ but $(\alpha+\beta) = 0$, the $Q$ satisfies the following fixed-point proportionality relationship
    \begin{equation}
        q_i \propto \left[ \sum_{r=1}^m \lambda_r g_r(a_i) + \dfrac{q_i^{1/\alpha-1} (n\Psi'(0) - \alpha\Psi'(c_2(q))) }{e^{-c_2(q)/\alpha}} \right]^{-1}.
        \label{eqn:maxent-dist-type-3}        
    \end{equation}

    In all of the above, the unknown constants $\lambda_1, \dots, \lambda_r$ are uniquely determined by the constraints given in~\eqref{eqn:maxent-constraints}.
\end{theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:maxent-dist} may be regarded as a generalization of the results on the maximum entropy distribution obtained by Ghosh and Basu~\cite{ghosh2021scale}. Specifically, with the choice of $\Psi(x) = x$ for the logarithmic Alpha-Beta divergence, we find that $\Psi'(x)=1$ and $\Psi''(x) = 0$ for any $x \in \R$, and hence the self-proportionality relationships given in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:maxent-dist-type-1}-\eqref{eqn:maxent-dist-type-3} becomes extremely simplified, namely one can obtain a closed form solutions to all of these fixed-point proportionality equations. 

In general, it is not known whether such a fixed-point proportionality relationship has a solution or if the solution is unique (up to a constant scaling factor) even if it exists. However, under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:gab-entropy-concave}, there is a guarantee of the concavity of the GAB entropy, which assures the uniqueness of the maximum entropy distribution, provided that the constraints~\eqref{eqn:maxent-constraints} have a feasible solution. Under such scenarios, one may start with any feasible solution $P_0$, compute its $\alpha$-escort distribution $Q_0$, and proceed by refining the estimate of $Q$ by carrying out one of the fixed-point equations~\eqref{eqn:maxent-dist-type-1}-\eqref{eqn:maxent-dist-type-3} iteratively until convergence.


\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

The generalized Alpha-Beta divergence introduced in this paper establishes a unifying framework for deriving new statistical divergence measures, which may find broader applications in robust statistics, signal processing, machine learning and pattern recognition. We derive its characterizing properties, and demonstrate how some fundamental properties are shared by all members of this broad family of divergence. We also indicate its associated entropy measure, which has many utilities in information theory. 

While we indicate a general method to construct new divergence measures of this family in Section~\ref{sec:gab-construct}, more extensive studies are needed to understand which of them have strong robustness properties and may prove useful in statistical inference problems. Extending on the Pythagorean theorem established in this paper, one may consider deriving the properties of forward projections and the reverse projections, which have interesting applications in hypothesis testing and model selection. Additionally, just as the R\'{e}yni entropy or Tsallis entropy arise naturally in modelling different physical phenomenons, further studies are needed to understand the natural appeal and the practical utility of the proposed generalized form of entropy. We expect to tackle these problems in the future.


