\section{Conclusions \& Policy implications}
\label{conclusion}
%Cappello
In this paper, we introduce a novel model for quantifying the impact of physical climate risk on an equity portfolio, utilizing a firm value model (FVM) approach.

%Results
We introduce a new and easily implementable modeling framework for estimating physical climate risk at the firm level, which can be seamlessly integrated into financial institutions' decision-making processes and customized for various climate risk metrics. The approach extends the multivariate Vasicek model in \eqref{eq:MultiMertonSDE_Sol} by incorporating negative jumps to account for climate-related losses. An explicit formula for the stressed equity value, based on the characteristic function of the jump process, is provided in \eqref{eq:option price}.



We calibrate the model using estimated climate risk losses in conjunction with widely available balance sheet data. Companies are clustered according to their physical risk profiles by leveraging their geographical vulnerability and asset intensity scores. This results in two clusters based on asset vulnerability and four clusters corresponding to increasing levels of asset intensity (cf. Figures \ref{fig:Distribution_of_clusters}–\ref{fig:Distribution_of_clusters_int_macro} and Tables \ref{table:Vulnerability_Table_Cluster_2}–\ref{table:Intensity_Table_Cluster}). The FVM parameters are then calibrated for each cluster by applying the two-step procedure outlined in Section \eqref{sect:FVMEstimation}.


We apply the calibrated model to compute the differences in Value-at-Risk (VaR) and expected loss between a baseline scenario and a climate-stressed scenario across various portfolios (see Table \ref{table:Alphas} and Figure \ref{fig:MSRLUTI}). Building on the VaR computation, we propose an add-on to the portfolio VaR that explicitly accounts for physical climate risk (see Figure \ref{fig:comp_add_on}). This framework highlights the heterogeneous exposure to climate risk across sectors such as utilities, which exhibit higher vulnerability.

%Policy implications
From a policy perspective, our framework facilitates the calculation of a VaR add-on for any equity portfolio, given knowledge of each stock's geographical location, sector and widely available balance sheet data. This enables regulators to define standardized VaR add-ons that could be potentially integrated into the Basel III standard approach, without requiring supervised entities to implement or replicate the model independently.

In summary, these results address the practical needs of financial institutions and respond to regulatory expectations—such as those set forth in the Basel framework—to effectively incorporate physical climate risks into risk management practices.





