\section*{Appendix}

\subsection*{Critical points of group-based models}

In building our typology of group-based models, we introduced a nested hierarchy of mathematical descriptions aimed at capturing different dimensions of groups. By moving from mean-field models to group-based models, one can capture the persistence of group members, adjust coupling between groups, or introduce group-level features. Typically, these different modeling approaches are solved with different methods, such as explicitly solving for the fixed points of mean-field models, or for the detailed balance of master equations. However, our nested descriptions can all be solved with a common perturbative approach. While our typology and main text are aimed at a wide audience, this appendix presents the perturbative approach for the specialized readers interested in the physics and dynamics of group-based models.

Here, we show how to compute the invasion threshold separating the inactive absorbing phase, where every agent is inactive/susceptible/defective, from an active one where active/infected/cooperative agents are sustained. This is done by studying the impact of small perturbations around the inactive absorbing phase. More precisely, expressing the activation rate as $\beta(n,i) = \lambda \tilde{\beta}(n,i)$, we want to find the critical value $\lambda_\text{cr}$ such that, at equilibrium, the populations is fully inactive for $\lambda < \lambda_\text{cr}$ and has non-zero activity for $\lambda > \lambda_\text{cr}$. Given the formal symmetry between activation $\beta(n,i)$ and deactivation $\alpha(n,i)$ rates in the models' equations, the calculation of the invasion threshold for deactivation (separating a fully active phase from an inactive one) follows through identical steps. We thus only present the derivation of the invasion threshold for activation, $\lambda_\text{cr}$.

For the model in \hyperref[tcolorbox:modelbox1]{Box 1}, a general closed form solution is accessible for $\lambda_\text{cr}$. The models in \hyperref[tcolorbox:modelbox2]{Box 2} to \hyperref[tcolorbox:modelbox4]{Box 4} allow for an implicit solution when deactivation (or activation) is an interaction-independent process. The recipe for obtaining the implicit solution is formally the same in all three cases: linearizing the equations, imposing stationarity, and finally relating the invasion threshold to the largest eigenvalue of a matrix associated with the obtained linear system by leveraging the Perron-Frobenius theorem.

\subsubsection*{\hyperref[tcolorbox:modelbox1]{Box 1}}

Let us first consider the case where both activation and deactivation are interaction-based processes. Close to the inactive absorbing state, we can expand $dI/dt$ from Eq.~(\ref{eq:ephemeral_1}) around $I=0$ to find, at leading order,
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d}{dt}I(t) = I(t)[\langle n(n-1)\beta(n,1)\rangle - \langle n\alpha(n,1)\rangle] \; ,
\end{equation}
where $\langle x\rangle$ denotes the average of $x$ over the group size distribution $p_n$. This is immediately solved to give, for small $I$, 
\begin{equation}
    I(t) = \exp\{[\langle n(n-1)\beta(n,1)\rangle - \langle n\alpha(n,1)\rangle]t\} \; .
\end{equation}
The small perturbation $I(t)$ will thus either grow or shrink exponentially depending on whether the argument of the exponential is positive or negative, respectively. Imposing that argument to be zero, using $\beta(n,i) = \lambda \tilde{\beta}(n,i)$ (and imposing $\tilde{\beta}(n,1)=1$ without loss of generality), we thus find
\begin{equation}
    \lambda_\text{cr} = \frac{\langle n\alpha(n,1)\rangle}{\langle n(n-1)\rangle} \; .
\end{equation}

Proceeding in the same way for the case where deactivation is interaction-independent, the linearization of Eq.~(\ref{eq:ephemeral_2}) eventually yields $\lambda_\text{cr} = 1/\langle n(n-1)\rangle$.


\subsubsection*{\hyperref[tcolorbox:modelbox2]{Box 2}}

Assuming deactivation is an interaction-independent process, the dynamical equations take the form
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d}{dt}G_{n,i} = (n-i+1)\left[\beta(n,i-1) + \rho\phi\right]G_{n,i-1}
    - (n-i)\left[\beta(n,i) + \rho\phi\right]G_{n,i} + (i+1)\alpha G_{n,i+1} - i\alpha G_{n,i} \; ,
    \label{eq:box2_app}
\end{equation}
with $\phi$ given in Eq.~(\ref{eq:box2_aux}), for $n \in \{n_\text{min},\dots,n_\text{max}\}$ and $i \in \{0,\dots,n\}$.

We linearize Eqs.~(\ref{eq:box2_app}) around the inactive state $I=0$, being $I = \sum_{i,n} (i/n)G_{n,i}$ the fraction of active agents per group, via the expansion
\begin{equation}
    G_{n,i} = G_n\delta_{i,0} + g_{n,i} I + {\cal O}(I^2) \; ,
    \label{eq:G_expansion}
\end{equation}
where $G_n \equiv \sum_i G_{n,i}$ denotes the fraction of groups of size $n$. The variables $\{g_{n,i}\}$ must satisfy $\sum_{n,i}(i/n)g_{n,i} = 1$ from the definition of $I$, while $\phi = I \sum_{n,i} (n-i)\beta(n,i)g_{n,i}/\langle n\rangle + {\cal O}(I^2)$, assumed $\beta(n,0)=0$ $\forall n$ to guarantee the inactive state to be absorbing. To the lowest order in $I$, after conveniently dividing both sides by $I$, Eqs.~(\ref{eq:box2_app}) become
\begin{align}
    \frac{1}{I}\frac{d}{dt}G_{n,1} =&~ \rho\frac{n}{\langle n\rangle}\sum_{n,i} (n-i)\beta(n,i)g_{n,i} - (n-1)\beta(n,1)g_{n,1} + 2\alpha g_{n,2} - \alpha g_{n,1} \; ,
    \label{eq:box2_app_lin1} \\
    \frac{1}{I}\frac{d}{dt}G_{n,i > 1} =&~ (n-i+1)\beta(n,i-1)g_{n,i-1} - (n-i)\beta(n,i)g_{n,i} + (i+1)\alpha g_{n,i+1} - i\alpha g_{n,i} \; ,
    \label{eq:box2_app_lin2}
\end{align}
where we omitted the equation for $dG_{n,0}/dt$ $\forall n$ as only $n$ of the $n+1$ equations are linearly independent due to the conservation of the total probability $dG_n/dt = 0$.

Now, since right at the invasion threshold the small perturbation $I$ neither grows nor shrinks, we can impose $dG_{n,i}/dt = 0$ $\forall\{n,i\}$. In this way, we obtain a determined linear system of $M=\sum_{n=n_\text{min}}^{n_\text{max}} n$ unknowns, i.e., $\{g_{n,i}\}_{n,i>0}$. This can be immediately recast in the form of an eigenvalue problem,
\begin{equation}
    \vec{g} = A\cdot\vec{g} \; ,
\end{equation}
where $\vec{g} \equiv (g_{n_\text{min},1},\dots,g_{n_\text{min},n_\text{min}},\dots,g_{n,1},\dots,g_{n,n},\dots,g_{n_\text{max},1},\dots,g_{n_\text{max},n_\text{max}})$ and $A$ is a non-negative and irreducible $M\times M$ matrix. From the Perron-Frobenius theorem \cite{meyer_matrix_2000}, $A$ admits only one physical eigenvector $\vec{g^*}$ of real, non-negative entries and such eigenvector is associated to its largest eigenvalue, $\Lambda_\text{max}(A)$. Therefore, the critical condition for the invasion threshold is
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda_\text{max}(A) = 1 \; .
\end{equation}
Finally, using $\beta(n,i) = \lambda \tilde{\beta}(n,i)$, the invasion threshold $\lambda_\text{cr}$ is found as the smallest value of $\lambda$ for which $\Lambda_\text{max}(A) = 1$ admits a solution that satisfies $\sum_{n,i} (i/n) g_{n,i} = 1$.

Notice that alternative derivations of an implicit solution for the invasion threshold for this model already exist \cite{hebert-dufresne_propagation_2010, st-onge_master_2021}.

\subsubsection*{\hyperref[tcolorbox:modelbox3]{Box 3}}

Assuming deactivation is an interaction-independent process, the dynamical equations take the form
\begin{align}
    \notag \frac{d}{dt}G_{n,i}^{\ell} =&~ (n-i+1)\left[\beta(n,i-1,\ell) + \rho\phi\right]G_{n,i-1}^{\ell}
    - (n-i)\left[\beta(n,i,\ell) + \rho\phi\right]G_{n,i}^{\ell} + (i+1)\alpha G_{n,i+1}^{\ell} - i\alpha G_{n,i}^{\ell} \\
    & + \nu_+(n,i,\ell-1) G_{n,i}^{\ell-1} + \nu_-(n,i,\ell+1) G_{n,i}^{\ell+1} - \left[\nu_-(n,i,\ell) + \nu_+(n,i,\ell)\right] G_{n,i}^{\ell} \; ,
    \label{eq:box3_app}
\end{align}
for $n \in \{n_\text{min},\dots,n_\text{max}\}$, $i \in \{0,\dots,n\}$, $\ell \in \{0,\dots,L\}$. We introduced two generic functions $\nu_\pm(n,i,\ell)$ representing the transition rates from level $\ell$ to $\ell\pm 1$ (for given $n$ and $i$), assumed to satisfy $\nu_\pm(n,i,\ell) = \tilde{\nu}_\pm(n,i,\ell) + {\cal O}(I)$ as the fraction $I = \sum_{n,i,\ell} (i/n)G_{n,i}^{\ell}$ of active agents per group in the population goes to zero. The specific model in \hyperref[tcolorbox:modelbox3]{Box 3} is recovered by setting $\nu_\pm(n,i,\ell) = h(n,i,\ell) Z^{\ell\pm1}_{n,i}/Z^{\ell}_{n,i}$.

To transform Eqs.~(\ref{eq:box3_app}) into a linear system we proceed by linearizing them around $I=0$ through the expansion
\begin{equation}
    G_{n,i}^{\ell} = G_n^{\ell} \delta_{i,0} + g_{n,i}^{\ell} I + {\cal O}(I^2) \; ,
    \label{eq:G_expansion_2}
\end{equation}
where we defined the fraction of groups of size $n$ and trait $\ell$, $G_n^{\ell} \equiv \sum_i G_{n,i}^\ell$. We have the relation $\sum_{n,i,\ell} (i/n) g_{n,i}^{\ell} = 1$ from the definition of $I$. Also, $\phi = I \sum_{n,i,\ell}(n-i)\beta(n,i,\ell)g_{n,i}^{\ell}/\langle n\rangle + {\cal O}(I^2)$, assumed $\beta(n,0,\ell)=0$ $\forall \{n,\ell\}$ to guarantee the inactive state to be absorbing. To the lowest order in $I$, Eqs.~(\ref{eq:box3_app}) thus become
\begin{align}
    \frac{d}{dt}G_{n,0}^{\ell} =&~ \tilde{\nu}_+(n,0,\ell-1) G_n^{\ell-1} + \tilde{\nu}_-(n,0,\ell+1) G_n^{\ell+1} - \left[\tilde{\nu}_-(n,0,\ell)+\tilde{\nu}_+(n,0,\ell)\right] G_n^{\ell} \label{eq:box3_app_lin1} \\
    \notag \frac{1}{I}\frac{d}{dt}G_{n,1}^{\ell} =&~ \rho\frac{n}{\langle n\rangle} \sum_{n,i,\ell}(n-i)\beta(n,i,\ell)g_{n,i}^{\ell} - (n-1)\beta(n,1,\ell)g_{n,1}^{\ell} + 2\alpha g_{n,2}^{\ell} - \alpha g_{n,1}^{\ell} \\
    &+ \tilde{\nu}_+(n,1,\ell-1) g_{n,1}^{\ell-1} + \tilde{\nu}_-(n,1,\ell+1) g_{n,1}^{\ell+1} - \left[\tilde{\nu}_-(n,1,\ell)+\tilde{\nu}_+(n,1,\ell)\right] g_{n,1}^{\ell} \label{eq:box3_app_lin2} \\
    \notag \frac{1}{I}\frac{d}{dt}G_{n,i > 1}^{\ell} =&~ (n-i+1)\beta(n,i-1,\ell)g_{n,i-1}^{\ell} - (n-i)\beta(n,i,\ell)g_{n,i}^{\ell} + (i+1)\alpha g_{n,i+1}^{\ell} - i\alpha g_{n,i}^{\ell} \\
    &+ \tilde{\nu}_+(n,i,\ell-1) g_{n,i}^{\ell-1} + \tilde{\nu}_-(n,i,\ell+1) g_{n,i}^{\ell+1} - \left[\tilde{\nu}_-(n,i,\ell)+\tilde{\nu}_+(n,i,\ell)\right]  g_{n,i}^{\ell}
    \; .
    \label{eq:box3_app_lin3}
\end{align}

At the invasion threshold the small perturbation $I$ neither grows nor shrinks, thus we impose $dG_{n,i}^\ell/dt = 0$ $\forall \{n,i,\ell\}$. First, Eq.~(\ref{eq:box3_app_lin1}) provides a closed linear system in the variables $\{G_n^\ell\}$ from which, by requiring detailed balance (i.e., that the transition rates between adjacent states are equal), we find the recursion $G_n^{\ell} = G_n^{\ell-1} \tilde{\nu}_+(n,0,\ell-1)/\tilde{\nu}_-(n,0,\ell)$. By iteration, we get
\begin{equation}
    G_n^\ell = G_n^0 \prod_{\ell'=1}^{\ell} \frac{\tilde{\nu}_+(n,0,\ell'-1)}{\tilde{\nu}_-(n,0,\ell')} \; ,
    \label{eq:box3_app_Gl}
\end{equation}
where $G_n^0 = 1 - \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} G_n^\ell$, from which
\begin{equation}
    G_n^0 = \left[1 + \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \prod_{\ell'=1}^{\ell} \frac{\tilde{\nu}_+(n,0,\ell'-1)}{\tilde{\nu}_-(n,0,\ell')}\right]^{-1} \; .
    \label{eq:box3_app_G0}
\end{equation}

Knowing the proportions $\{G_n^\ell\}$, we now set the left-hand side of Eqs.~(\ref{eq:box3_app_lin2}) and (\ref{eq:box3_app_lin3}) to zero to get a closed linear system in the variables $\{g_{n,i}^\ell\}$. We can easily rearrange Eqs.~(\ref{eq:box3_app_lin2}) and (\ref{eq:box3_app_lin3}) to express each $g_{n,i > 1}^\ell$ as a linear combination of its adjacent states ($g_{n,i \pm 1}^\ell$, $g_{n,i}^{\ell \pm 1}$), and each $g_{n,1}^\ell$ as a linear combination of potentially all other states (due to the sum in the right-hand side of Eq.~(\ref{eq:box3_app_lin2})). Defining the $M$-dimensional vector $\vec{g} \equiv (\dots,g_{n,1}^0,\dots,g_{n,n}^0,g_{n,1}^1,\dots,g_{n,n}^1,\dots,g_{n,1}^L,\dots,g_{n,n}^L,\dots)_{n=n_\text{min}}^{n_\text{max}}$, where $M = \sum_{n=n_\text{min}}^{n_\text{max}} n(L+1)$, the resulting linear system can be recast in the eigenvalue problem $\vec{g} = A\cdot\vec{g}$, where $A$ is an $M\times M$ non-negative and irreducible matrix. Applying the Perron-Frobenius theorem as before, we conclude that the critical condition for the invasion threshold is $\Lambda_\text{max}(A) = 1$. Using $\beta(n,i) = \lambda \tilde{\beta}(n,i)$, the invasion threshold $\lambda_\text{cr}$ is found as the smallest value of $\lambda$ for which $\Lambda_\text{max}(A) = 1$ admits a solution that satisfies $\sum_{n,i,\ell} (i/n) g_{n,i}^{\ell} = 1$.

\subsubsection*{\hyperref[tcolorbox:modelbox4]{Box 4}}

Assuming deactivation is an interaction-independent process, the dynamical equations take the form
\begin{align}
    \notag \frac{d}{dt}G_{n,i}^{\ell} =&~ (n-i+1)\left[\gamma f_a(\ell) + \beta(n,i-1,\ell) + \rho\phi\right]G_{n,i-1}^{\ell}
    - (n-i)\left[\gamma f_a(\ell) + \beta(n,i,\ell) + \rho\phi\right]G_{n,i}^{\ell} \\
    \notag & + (i+1)[\gamma f_d(\ell) + \alpha]G_{n,i+1}^{\ell} - i[\gamma f_d(\ell) + \alpha]G_{n,i}^{\ell} \\
    \notag & + h_+(n,i,\ell)[\mu+\rho\nu_+(n,i,\ell-1)] G_{n,i}^{\ell-1} + [\mu h_-(n,i,\ell+1)+\rho\nu_-(n,i,\ell+1)] G_{n,i}^{\ell+1} \\
    &- [\mu h_-(n,i,\ell)+\rho\nu_-(n,i,\ell)] G_{n,i}^{\ell} - h_+(n,i,\ell+1)[\mu+\rho\nu_+(n,i,\ell)] G_{n,i}^{\ell} \; .
    \label{eq:box4_app}
\end{align}
for $n \in \{n_\text{min},\dots,n_\text{max}\}$, $i \in \{0,\dots,n\}$, $\ell \in \{0,\dots,L\}$. The equation above generalizes the model in \hyperref[tcolorbox:modelbox4]{Box 4} by introducing the generic functions (i) $f_a$ and $f_d$ of spontaneous individual activation and deactivation, (ii) $h_\pm(n,i,\ell)$, relating the transition rate to/from level $\ell$ from/to level $\ell- 1$ to the cost needed to upgrade to/sustain level $\ell$, and (iii) as in the previous section, $\nu_\pm(n,i,\ell)$ for the transition rate from trait $\ell$ to traits $\ell\pm 1$ due to inter-group interactions (again assumed to satisfy $\nu_\pm(n,i,\ell) = \tilde{\nu}_\pm(n,i,\ell) + {\cal O}(I)$ as the fraction $I$ of active agents per group in the population goes to zero).

We linearize around the \emph{free} state defined here as the one where every agent is inactive ($I=0$) and all groups have trait $\ell=0$ ($G_n^\ell\propto\delta_{\ell,0}$), and require $f_a(0) = 0$ and $h_+(n,0,\ell) = 0$ $\forall \ell$ (in addition to $\beta(n,0,\ell)=0$ $\forall \{n,\ell\}$) to guarantee the free state to be absorbing.

Generalizing what done in the previous section, we linearize Eqs.~(\ref{eq:box4_app}) around the free state through Eq.~(\ref{eq:G_expansion_2}) and $G_n^\ell = G_n\delta_{\ell,0} + q_n^\ell I + {\cal O}(I^2)$, to get the expansion
\begin{equation}
    G_{n,i}^{\ell} = G_n\delta_{\ell,0} \delta_{i,0} + (q_n^\ell \delta_{i,0} + g_{n,i}^{\ell}) I + {\cal O}(I^2) \; ,
    \label{eq:G_expansion_3}
\end{equation}
being $G_n \equiv \sum_{i,\ell} G_{n,i}^\ell$ the proportion of groups of size $n$. The relations $\sum_{n,i,\ell} (i/n) g_{n,i}^{\ell} = 1$ and $\phi = I \sum_{n,i,\ell}(n-i)\beta(n,i,\ell)g_{n,i}^{\ell}/\langle n\rangle + {\cal O}(I^2)$ hold. To the lowest order in $I$, Eqs.~(\ref{eq:box4_app}) become
\begin{align}
    \frac{1}{I}\frac{d}{dt}G_{n,0}^{0} =& -\rho\frac{n}{\langle n\rangle} \sum_{n,i,\ell}(n-i)\beta(n,i,\ell)g_{n,i}^{\ell} + [\gamma f_d(0)+\alpha]g_{n,1}^{0} + [\mu h_-(n,0,1)+\rho\tilde{\nu}_-(n,0,1)](q_n^1 + g_{n,0}^{1}) \; ,
    \label{eq:box4_app_lin1} \\
    \notag \frac{1}{I}\frac{d}{dt}G_{n,1}^{0} =& ~\rho\frac{n}{\langle n\rangle} \sum_{n,i,\ell}(n-i)\beta(n,i,\ell)g_{n,i}^{\ell} - (n-1)\beta(n,1,0)g_{n,1}^{0} + 2[\gamma f_d(0)+\alpha] g_{n,2}^{0} - [\gamma f_d(0)+\alpha] g_{n,1}^{0} \\
    &+ [\mu h_-(n,1,1)+\rho \tilde{\nu}_-(n,1,1)]g_{n,1}^{1} - h_+(n,1,1)[\mu +\rho \tilde{\nu}_+(n,1,0)]g_{n,1}^{0} \; , \label{eq:box4_app_lin2} \\
    \notag \frac{1}{I}\frac{d}{dt}G_{n,0}^{\ell > 0} =& -n \gamma f_a(\ell)(q_n^\ell + g_{n,0}^{\ell}) + [\gamma f_d(\ell)+\alpha]g_{n,1}^{\ell} \\
    &+ [\mu h_-(n,0,\ell+1)+\rho \tilde{\nu}_-(n,0,\ell+1)](q_n^{\ell+1}+g_{n,0}^{\ell+1}) - [\mu h_-(n,0,\ell)+\rho \tilde{\nu}_-(n,0,\ell)](q_n^{\ell}+g_{n,0}^{\ell})\; ,
    \label{eq:box4_app_lin3} \\
    \notag \frac{1}{I}\frac{d}{dt}G_{n,1}^{\ell > 0} =&~ n \gamma f_a(\ell)(q_n^\ell + g_{n,0}^{\ell}) - (n-1)[\gamma f_a(\ell)+\beta(n,1,\ell)]g_{n,1}^{\ell} + 2[\gamma f_d(\ell)+\alpha]g_{n,2}^{\ell} - [\gamma f_d(\ell)+\alpha]g_{n,1}^{\ell} \\
    \notag &+ h_+(n,1,\ell)[\mu +\rho \tilde{\nu}_+(n,1,\ell-1)]g_{n,1}^{\ell-1} - h_+(n,1,\ell+1)[\mu +\rho \tilde{\nu}_+(n,1,\ell)]g_{n,1}^{\ell} \\
    &+ [\mu h_-(n,1,\ell+1)+\rho \tilde{\nu}_-(n,1,\ell+1)]g_{n,1}^{\ell+1} - [\mu h_-(n,1,\ell)+\rho \tilde{\nu}_-(n,1,\ell)]g_{n,1}^{\ell} \; ,
    \label{eq:box4_app_lin4} \\
    \notag \frac{1}{I}\frac{d}{dt}G_{n,i>1}^{\ell} =&~ (n-i+1)[\gamma f_a(\ell)+\beta(n,i-1,\ell)]g_{n,i-1}^{\ell} \\
    \notag &-(n-i)[\gamma f_a(\ell)+\beta(n,i,\ell)]g_{n,i}^{\ell} + (i+1)[\gamma f_d(\ell)+\alpha] g_{n,i+1}^{\ell} - i[\gamma f_d(\ell)+\alpha] g_{n,i}^{\ell} \\
    \notag &+[\mu h_-(n,i,\ell+1)+\rho \tilde{\nu}_-(n,i,\ell+1)]g_{n,i}^{\ell+1} - [\mu h_-(n,i,\ell)+\rho \tilde{\nu}_-(n,i,\ell)]g_{n,i}^{\ell} \\
    &+h_+(n,i,\ell)[\mu +\rho \tilde{\nu}_+(n,i,\ell-1)]g_{n,i}^{\ell-1} - h_+(n,i,\ell+1)[\mu +\rho \tilde{\nu}_+(n,i,\ell)]g_{n,i}^{\ell} \; .
    \label{eq:box4_app_lin5}
\end{align}

As before, since at the invasion threshold the small perturbation $I$ neither grows nor shrinks, we impose $dG_{n,i}^\ell/dt = 0$ $\forall \{n,i,\ell\}$. We first observe that $q_n^\ell$ and $g_{n,0}^{\ell}$ always appear summed together. Defining $\tilde{g}_{n,0}^{\ell} \equiv q_n^\ell + g_{n,0}^{\ell}$, we thus obtain a determined linear system of $M = (L+1)\sum_{n=n_\text{min}}^{n_\text{max}}(n+1)-\sum_{n=n_\text{min}}^{n_\text{max}}1 = (L+1)\sum_{n=n_\text{min}}^{n_\text{max}}n +\sum_{n=n_\text{min}}^{n_\text{max}}L$ independent equations (one constraint for each value taken by $n$ being probability conservation, i.e., $d(\sum_{i,\ell} G_{n,i}^\ell)/dt=0$) of $M$ unknowns, i.e., $\{g_{n,i}^{\ell}\}^{\ell=0,\dots,L}_{n=n_\text{min}\dots,n_\text{max};\ i=1,\dots,n}$ plus $\{\tilde{g}_{n,0}^{\ell}\}_{n=n_\text{min}\dots,n_\text{max}}^{\ell=1,\dots,L}$. We can finally recast the system in the form $\vec{g}=A \cdot \vec{g}$, being $\vec{g} \equiv (\dots,g_{n,1}^0,\dots,g_{n,n}^0,g_{n,1}^1,\dots,g_{n,n}^1,\dots,g_{n,1}^L,\dots,g_{n,n}^L,\dots,\tilde{g}_{n,0}^1,\dots,\tilde{g}_{n,0}^L,\dots)_{n=n_\text{min}}^{n_\text{max}}$ and $A$ an $M\times M$ non-negative and irreducible matrix. Applying the Perron-Frobenius theorem and using $\beta(n,i) = \lambda \tilde{\beta}(n,i)$, the invasion threshold $\lambda_\text{cr}$ is found as the smallest value of $\lambda$ for which $\Lambda_\text{max}(A) = 1$ admits a solution that satisfies $\sum_{n,i,\ell} (i/n) g_{n,i}^{\ell} = 1$.

