\section{Introduction}\label{sec-intro}

Data has become instrumental to all facets of life in the 21st century.
Data acumen is a foundational set of skills and understandings that an
individual should have to effectively leverage data science
\citep{NAP25104}. K--12 school districts have, to varying degrees, begun
to adapt their curricula to teach data-related skills to students
\citep{rosenberg_big_2022, nasemwork:2023}. The question of how best to
foster data acumen in K--12 students remains unsolved.

Spreadsheets serve as one of multiple tools to help students develop
this skill set. While there are limitations to what spreadsheets can
accomplish in terms of data analysis and modeling \citep{nashjohn:1996},
they provide a foundation for data skills and are an important part of a
data science workflow.

Spreadsheets are already part of K--12 data science education. A sample
of 330 K--12 science educators showed 89\% use a spreadsheet software
(either Google Sheets or Excel) with their students
\citep{rosenberg_big_2022}. Over 90\% of these educators use either
Google Sheets or both tools.

Past work has considered guidelines and best practices surrounding the
use of spreadsheets for data science. For instance,
\citet{hertz_eleven_2024} provide a number of tips to effectively
utilize a spreadsheet tool for analysis, focused on the importance of
making spreadsheets machine-readable (including by having one data point
per cell), using computer-friendly characters, defining null values, and
being thoughtful around datetime records. \citet{brom:woo:2018} share
detailed guidance for how data should be organized in spreadsheets. They
call for consistency, a thoughtful choice of names for columns and
files, a prohibition on calculations within a data spreadsheet, and a
plea that data be stored in a rectangular format \citep{wick:2014}.

\citet{piment:2022} reviewed attributes of existing data science tools
and technologies at the K--12 level. They evaluated data science tools
against a slightly modified framework for statistics tools originally
proposed by \citet{mcnamara_key_2018}. Although \citet{piment:2022}
mention multiple merits and drawbacks of spreadsheets, they eventually
conclude that, ``relying solely on spreadsheets as a data analysis tool
is likely to lead to a frustrating experience for those learning and
engaging in data science.''

No one is suggesting that sole reliance on any one tool is sufficient
for a data science workflow. One goal of this paper is to identify ways
that spreadsheets can be part of a toolkit to help students develop data
skills. We seek to demonstrate the utility of spreadsheets in a K--12
data science curriculum and provide educators and curriculum developers
with relevant learning outcomes and approaches that may facilitate a
curricular shift to expand data science education.

Toward these goals, we first evaluate spreadsheets against existing
frameworks to better assess their advantages and limitations. After
addressing \emph{how} K--12 students might be taught data acumen skills,
we turn to a discussion of \emph{what} a successful, holistic curriculum
would lead a student to walk away with. This includes a proposed list of
five learning outcomes and ideas for the integration of such skills into
different types of classes. Finally, we outline challenges, concerns,
limitations, and tangible benefits of teaching with spreadsheets in this
way.

