\section{Ultradiscretization}

To derive the ultradiscrete max-plus equation for eq.(\ref{eqn:tropical_Ludwing_limit}), 
%
the variable transformations
%
\begin{eqnarray}
    x_n=e^{X_n/\varepsilon},~r=e^{R/\varepsilon},~k=e^{K/\varepsilon},
    \label{transformation}
\end{eqnarray}
%
are applied.
%
Then, the ultradiscrete limit
\[
\lim_{\varepsilon \to +0}\varepsilon\log(e^{A/\varepsilon}+e^{B/\varepsilon})=\max(A,B)	
\]
provides the following max-plus equation,
%
%\begin{eqnarray}
%    X_{n+1}=\max (X_n,T+R+X_n)-\max\left[0,T+\max(X_n+R-K,X_n-\max(0,2X_n))\right].    
%    \label{eqn:2-1}
%\end{eqnarray}
%
%Assuming the condition
%
%$T \leq \max(-R,K-R-X_n)$,
%
%we obtain the ultradiscrete max-plus model of the Ludwing model (\ref{eqn:00}) as
%
%
\begin{eqnarray}
    X_{n+1}=R-\max(R-K,-\max(0,2X_n)).    
    \label{eqn:UD_Ludwing}
\end{eqnarray}
%   
%Note that the parameters $R$ and $K$ correspond to $r$ and $k$, respectively.
%
The dynamical properties of eq.(\ref{eqn:UD_Ludwing}) 
can be understood by the graph analysis (cobweb plot)\cite{Ohmori2020}.
%
First we consider the case $R>K$.
%
Figure \ref{Fig.1} shows the graphs of eq.(\ref{eqn:UD_Ludwing}) for (a) $K>0$ and (b) $K<0$.
%
Since $\max (0, 2X_{n}) \geq 0$, 
eq.(\ref{eqn:UD_Ludwing}) becomes $X_{n+1} = K$ 
for any $X_{n}$.
%
Therefore, $X_n=K$ is the unique fixed point 
and is stable.
%
%
